COURSE_DATA["csc420"]={topics:[
{id:'t1',title:'Transactions and ACID',why:'BEGIN/COMMIT/ROLLBACK queries and ACID definitions appear in the practical test.',
cards:[
{h:'ACID',b:`<p>A <b>transaction</b> is a unit of work that executes completely or not at all.</p><table><tr><th>Property</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td><b>Atomicity</b></td><td>All or nothing</td></tr><tr><td><b>Consistency</b></td><td>Moves the DB from one valid state to another (constraints hold)</td></tr><tr><td><b>Isolation</b></td><td>Concurrent transactions do not interfere</td></tr><tr><td><b>Durability</b></td><td>Committed changes survive crashes (WAL)</td></tr></table>`},
{h:'SQL',b:`<pre>BEGIN;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 500 WHERE id = 1;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 500 WHERE id = 2;
COMMIT;   -- or ROLLBACK;

BEGIN;
INSERT INTO orders(customer_id, total) VALUES (3, 120);
SAVEPOINT s1;
DELETE FROM orders WHERE id = 99;
ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT s1;
COMMIT;</pre><p>Anomalies: dirty read, non-repeatable read, phantom read. Isolation levels: Read Uncommitted, <b>Read Committed</b> (PostgreSQL default), Repeatable Read, Serializable.</p>`},
{h:'Five transaction states & state transitions',b:`<p><b>Five transaction states (Lecture 1 Slide 8 &amp; Past Exam Q5a):</b></p><ul><li><b>1. Active:</b> the initial state; the transaction stays here while its statements are executing.</li><li><b>2. Partially committed:</b> after the final statement has executed (output may still be buffered in memory before disk flush).</li><li><b>3. Failed:</b> after the system discovers that normal execution can no longer proceed (e.g. hardware error, syntax error, or a violated CHECK/foreign-key constraint).</li><li><b>4. Aborted:</b> after the transaction has been rolled back and the database restored to its state prior to the start of the transaction. Two options follow:<ul><li><b>Restart the transaction:</b> only if the abort was caused by an external hardware/software error or deadlock (no internal logical error).</li><li><b>Kill the transaction:</b> if it failed due to an internal logical error (e.g. bad input or constraint violation).</li></ul></li><li><b>5. Committed:</b> after successful completion and permanent write to disk.</li></ul><div class="flow"><span>Active</span><em>→</em><span>Partially Committed</span><em>→</em><span>Committed</span></div><div class="flow"><span>Active / Partially Committed</span><em>→</em><span>Failed</span><em>→</em><span>Aborted (Restart or Kill)</span></div>`},
{h:'Constraint violations, concurrent locks & Write-Ahead Logging (WAL)',b:`<ul><li><b>Atomicity &amp; Consistency demo (Slides 11–14 &amp; Exam Q1a):</b> if a multi-statement transaction inserts a valid row (producing <code>INSERT 0 1</code>) and then a second statement violates a constraint (e.g. a foreign key to <code>'Zoology'</code> or a <code>CHECK (salary &gt;= 0)</code> constraint), the transaction moves to <b>Failed → Aborted</b> and rolls back <i>all</i> changes—even the earlier <code>INSERT 0 1</code> disappears!</li><li><b>Isolation &amp; Row Locks demo (Slides 15–18):</b> when User 1 runs <code>BEGIN; UPDATE course SET credits = 4 WHERE coursecode = 'CS-347';</code> without committing, User 2 still sees the old credit value (isolation). If User 2 tries to <code>UPDATE</code> the same row, User 2's session <b>blocks and waits</b> because User 1 holds a write lock on that row until User 1 runs <code>COMMIT</code> or <code>ROLLBACK</code>.</li><li><b>Durability &amp; Write-Ahead Logging (WAL, Slide 19):</b> PostgreSQL enforces durability by recording every change in the <b>WAL log on disk before</b> applying it to the actual data files. If Postgres crashes right after <code>COMMIT</code> returns, WAL replay on restart recovers every committed change.</li></ul>`}],
recall:[['Expand ACID.','Atomicity, consistency, isolation, durability.'],['What does ROLLBACK do?','Undoes all changes since BEGIN (or to a savepoint).'],['PostgreSQL default isolation level?','Read Committed.'],['Define a dirty read.','Reading uncommitted changes made by another transaction.'],['Name the five states of a database transaction and the transitions between them.','Active (executing) -> Partially committed (after final statement) -> Committed (permanent). From Active or Partially committed, an error moves the transaction to Failed -> Aborted (rolled back).'],['After a transaction enters the Aborted state, what are the two options and when is each used?','1. Restart the transaction (only if there was no internal logical error in the transaction itself, e.g. hardware/network glitch), or 2. Kill the transaction (when caused by an internal logical error or constraint violation).'],['In Lecture 1, if Statement 1 in a BEGIN...COMMIT block prints "INSERT 0 1" and Statement 2 fails a CHECK or FOREIGN KEY constraint, does Statement 1 remain in the table?','No. Because of Atomicity and Consistency, the entire transaction fails and is rolled back (Aborted), so Statement 1 is completely undone.'],['What happens when Session 2 tries to UPDATE a row that Session 1 has already UPDATED inside an uncommitted transaction?','Session 2 blocks (waits) because Session 1 holds an exclusive lock on the database row until Session 1 either commits or rolls back.'],['How does PostgreSQL implement Durability so committed changes survive an immediate power crash?','Through Write-Ahead Logging (WAL): every change is written to the WAL log before it is applied to data files, and Postgres replays the WAL on restart.']],
quiz:[{k:'mcq',q:'Which property ensures committed data survives a power failure?',o:['Atomicity','Consistency','Isolation','Durability'],a:3},{k:'gap',q:'To undo to a marked point, use ROLLBACK TO ___.',a:'savepoint',alt:['savepoint s1']},{k:'tf',q:'Serializable is the strictest isolation level.',a:true},{k:'mcq',q:'Which transaction state is entered right after the final statement has executed, before changes are permanently flushed to commit?',o:['Active','Partially committed','Failed','Aborted'],a:1,w:undefined},{k:'mcq',q:'When an aborted transaction failed because of an internal logical error (such as a negative salary violating a CHECK constraint), the system should…',o:['Automatically restart it in an infinite loop','Kill the transaction','Force a dirty commit','Drop the table'],a:1,w:undefined},{k:'mcq',q:'In two concurrent psql sessions, User 1 runs BEGIN; UPDATE course SET credits = 4 WHERE coursecode = \'CS-347\'; (not yet committed). What happens if User 2 tries to UPDATE the same row?',o:['User 2 overwrites User 1 immediately','User 2 blocks and waits because User 1 holds a lock on that row until COMMIT or ROLLBACK','PostgreSQL crashes','User 1 is logged out'],a:1,w:undefined},{k:'gap',q:'PostgreSQL guarantees durability by recording changes first in the Write-Ahead Log, abbreviated ___.',a:'wal',alt:['WAL'],w:undefined},{k:'tf',q:'If the first INSERT in a transaction prints "INSERT 0 1" and the second statement violates a CHECK constraint, the first row remains saved after rollback.',a:false,w:'Atomicity and Consistency undo the entire transaction, including the first INSERT.'}],
theory:[{q:'Write a transaction that transfers 1,000 from account 5 to account 8, and explain what happens if the second update fails.',pts:['BEGIN','Debit','Credit','COMMIT','ROLLBACK on failure','Atomicity explained'],model:`<pre>BEGIN;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 1000 WHERE id = 5;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 1000 WHERE id = 8;
COMMIT;</pre><p>If the credit fails, issue ROLLBACK: atomicity undoes the debit so money is not lost.</p>`},{q:'(Feb 2026 Exam Q1a & Q5a) Textually and pictorially describe the five possible states of a database transaction. Then write an SQL transaction to insert instructor (\'90001\', \'Stephen Adubi\', \'Comp. Sci.\', 120000.00) and add his salary to the Comp. Sci. department budget, and explain via ACID what happens if a negative salary (-120000) is submitted when a CHECK (salary >= 0) constraint exists.',pts:['5 transaction states defined: Active, Partially committed, Failed, Aborted (Restart vs Kill), Committed','State diagram arrows (Active -> Partially Committed -> Committed; Active/Partially Committed -> Failed -> Aborted)','SQL transaction block: BEGIN; INSERT INTO instructor...; UPDATE department SET budget = budget + 120000.00 WHERE deptname = \'Comp. Sci.\'; COMMIT;','Negative salary explanation: CHECK constraint violated -> Failed -> Aborted (ROLLBACK)','ACID link: Consistency (check constraint preserved) + Atomicity (neither instructor nor budget update persists) + Isolation/Durability','Final DB state: unchanged (identical to state before BEGIN)'],model:`<p><b>Background:</b> a database transaction is a logical unit of work that must satisfy the four <b>ACID</b> properties (Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability) while progressing through five well-defined lifecycle states.</p><p><b>Answer and solution:</b></p><ul><li><b>1. Five Transaction States &amp; Diagram (Exam Q5a):</b><ul><li><b>Active:</b> the initial state while statements are executing.</li><li><b>Partially Committed:</b> entered right after the final statement finishes executing in memory.</li><li><b>Committed:</b> entered after all changes are permanently flushed to disk (via WAL).</li><li><b>Failed:</b> entered from Active or Partially Committed as soon as a constraint violation, syntax error or hardware fault prevents normal completion.</li><li><b>Aborted:</b> entered after rollback restores the database to its pre-transaction state; the DBMS then either <b>restarts</b> the transaction (if no internal logical error) or <b>kills</b> it (if caused by bad logic/data).</li></ul><pre>           ┌──────────────────────► Partially Committed ──────► Committed
           │                                 │
Active ────┤                                 ▼
           └───────────────────────────►  Failed  ───────────► Aborted (Restart / Kill)</pre></li><li><b>2. Onboarding SQL Transaction Block (Exam Q1a-i):</b><pre>BEGIN;
INSERT INTO instructor (id, name, deptname, salary)
VALUES ('90001', 'Stephen Adubi', 'Comp. Sci.', 120000.00);

UPDATE department
SET budget = budget + 120000.00
WHERE deptname = 'Comp. Sci.';
COMMIT;</pre></li><li><b>3. Effect of Submitting a Negative Salary (Exam Q1a-ii):</b> if <code>-120000.00</code> is submitted, the <code>INSERT</code> violates the table's <code>CHECK (salary &gt;= 0)</code> constraint. Under <b>Consistency</b> (transactions must preserve all database integrity rules) and <b>Atomicity</b> (all-or-nothing execution), the transaction transitions from <b>Active → Failed → Aborted</b> (and is killed due to the logical input error). Even if the <code>UPDATE department</code> had run first, rollback undoes everything.</li></ul><p><b>Scenario &amp; Final Database State:</b> after the aborted transaction, the database remains in the exact state it was in prior to <code>BEGIN</code>—<code>'90001'</code> is not in <code>instructor</code> and the <code>'Comp. Sci.'</code> budget is unchanged.</p><p><b>How they combine:</b> the CHECK constraint detects the consistency breach, the state machine moves the transaction through Failed to Aborted, and Atomicity guarantees zero partial updates.</p><p><b>Conclusion:</b> wrapping multi-table onboarding updates inside <code>BEGIN ... COMMIT</code> ensures that instructor records and departmental budgets never drift out of sync.</p>`}]},
{id:'t2',title:'Indexing',why:'CREATE INDEX, EXPLAIN ANALYZE, expression indexes: in the practical paper.',
cards:[
{h:'Indexes',b:`<p>An <b>index</b> is a data structure that speeds up lookups at the cost of storage and slower writes. PostgreSQL default = <b>B-tree</b> (=, <, >, ORDER BY). Others: Hash (=), GIN (arrays, JSONB, full text), GiST.</p><pre>CREATE INDEX idx_customers_email ON customers(email);
CREATE INDEX idx_lower_email ON customers(LOWER(email));  -- expression index
CREATE UNIQUE INDEX ... ; DROP INDEX idx_customers_email;
EXPLAIN ANALYZE SELECT * FROM customers WHERE email = 'a@b.com';</pre><p>Without the index: <b>Seq Scan</b>; with it: <b>Index Scan</b> / Bitmap Index Scan. An expression index is used only if the query uses the same expression: <code>WHERE LOWER(email) = 'a@b.com'</code>.</p>`},
{h:'B+-tree complexity, node anatomy & range-query leaf chain',b:`<p><b>Complexity (Slide 21):</b> a full sequential scan costs $O(N)$, whereas searching a B+-tree costs $O(\\log_b N)$ where $b$ is the branching factor (e.g. for $b=10$: $100 \\text{ rows} \\to 2$ lookups; $1{,}000 \\to 3$; $1{,}000{,}000 \\to 6$; $1{,}000{,}000{,}000 \\to 9$). B+-trees automatically reorganise with small local changes on insert/delete.</p><p><b>Node structure of an order-$n$ (degree-$n$) B+-tree (Slides 24–26):</b></p><ul><li><b>Every node</b> holds up to $n - 1$ sorted search keys ($K_1 &lt; K_2 &lt; \\dots &lt; K_{n-1}$) and up to $n$ pointers ($P_1, \\dots, P_n$).</li><li><b>Non-leaf (internal) nodes:</b> form a multilevel sparse index. All keys in the subtree under $P_1$ are $&lt; K_1$; keys under $P_i$ satisfy $K_{i-1} \\le \\text{key} &lt; K_i$; keys under $P_m$ are $\\ge K_{m-1}$.</li><li><b>Leaf nodes:</b> hold up to $n - 1$ keys; each pointer $P_i$ points to the data record (or a bucket of pointers for duplicates), and the <b>last pointer $P_n$ links to the next leaf node in sorted left-to-right order</b>.</li><li><b>How a range query uses a B+-tree (Exam Q1c-ii):</b> to find all names <code>BETWEEN 'Alimi' AND 'Gold'</code>, Postgres traverses from the root down to the leaf holding <code>'Alimi'</code> in $O(\\log_b N)$ steps, then simply <b>follows the horizontal $P_n$ next-leaf pointers left-to-right</b> until passing <code>'Gold'</code>, without ever climbing back up to internal nodes!</li></ul>`},
{h:'Worked B+-tree insertion (Exam Q1c: degree n = 3)',b:`<p><b>Exam Q1c worked simulation:</b> B+-tree of degree $n = 3$ (each node holds at most $n - 1 = 2$ keys and $3$ pointers; splits occur on the 3rd key). Insert in order: <code>'Wu', 'Einstein', 'Katz', 'Singh', 'Alimi', 'Crick', 'Brandt', 'Gold'</code>:</p><ol><li><b>Insert 'Wu', then 'Einstein':</b> single leaf fills to 2 sorted keys: <code>[Einstein | Wu]</code>.</li><li><b>Insert 'Katz' (Split 1 — leaf overflow, 3 keys):</b> leaf has <code>{Einstein, Katz, Wu}</code>. Split into left leaf <code>[Einstein]</code> and right leaf <code>[Katz | Wu]</code>; promote the middle/right-first key <code>'Katz'</code> to a new root: Root <code>[Katz]</code>.</li><li><b>Insert 'Singh' (Split 2 — right leaf overflow):</b> goes to right leaf <code>{Katz, Singh, Wu}</code> (3 keys &gt; 2). Split into <code>[Katz]</code> and <code>[Singh | Wu]</code>; promote <code>'Singh'</code> to root: Root <code>[Katz | Singh]</code> with 3 leaves: <code>[Einstein] → [Katz] → [Singh | Wu]</code>.</li><li><b>Insert 'Alimi':</b> goes to leftmost leaf <code>[Alimi | Einstein]</code> (2 keys, no split).</li><li><b>Insert 'Crick' (Split 3 — leftmost leaf overflow + Split 4 — root overflow):</b> leftmost leaf has <code>{Alimi, Crick, Einstein}</code> → splits into <code>[Alimi]</code> and <code>[Crick | Einstein]</code>, pushing <code>'Crick'</code> up to root. Root now has 3 keys <code>{Crick, Katz, Singh}</code> (overflow!) → internal split promotes middle key <code>'Katz'</code> to a new top root <code>[Katz]</code> with left internal child <code>[Crick]</code> and right internal child <code>[Singh]</code>.</li><li><b>Insert 'Brandt':</b> goes to leaf <code>[Alimi | Brandt]</code> (2 keys, no split).</li><li><b>Insert 'Gold' (Split 5 — leaf [Crick | Einstein] overflow):</b> <code>'Gold' &lt; 'Katz'</code> and <code>&gt;= 'Crick'</code>, so it enters leaf <code>{Crick, Einstein, Gold}</code> → splits into <code>[Crick]</code> and <code>[Einstein | Gold]</code>, pushing <code>'Einstein'</code> into left internal node <code>[Crick | Einstein]</code> (2 keys, fits!).</li></ol><p><b>Final degree-3 B+-tree:</b> Top Root <code>[Katz]</code> → Level 1 internal nodes <code>[Crick | Einstein]</code> and <code>[Singh]</code> → 5 chained leaves: <code>[Alimi | Brandt] → [Crick] → [Einstein | Gold] → [Katz] → [Singh | Wu]</code>.</p>`},
{h:'What vs How to index, 6 index types, covering/partial & Bitmap scans',b:`<table><tr><th>"What" we index (Scope)</th><th>"How" we index (Access Method)</th></tr><tr><td>• <b>Single-column:</b> <code>ON t(col)</code><br>• <b>Composite (multi-column):</b> <code>ON t(c1, c2)</code> (most selective leading)<br>• <b>Covering (INCLUDE):</b> <code>ON ratings(rating) INCLUDE (movie_id, votes)</code> → enables <b>Index Only Scan</b> (<code>Heap Fetches: 0</code>)<br>• <b>Partial (filtered):</b> <code>ON ratings(votes) WHERE votes &gt; 100000</code><br>• <b>Functional / Expression:</b> <code>ON customers(LOWER(email))</code></td><td>• <b>B-tree (default):</b> <code>=, &lt;, &lt;=, &gt;, &gt;=, BETWEEN, ORDER BY, LIKE 'abc%'</code> ($O(\\log_b N)$)<br>• <b>Hash:</b> <code>USING hash(name)</code> — equality <code>=</code> only ($O(1)$)<br>• <b>GIN:</b> arrays (<code>@&gt;, &amp;&amp;</code>), JSONB (<code>?, @&gt;</code>), full-text<br>• <b>GiST:</b> geometric, ranges, nearest-neighbour<br>• <b>SP-GiST:</b> quad-trees, tries<br>• <b>BRIN:</b> huge naturally ordered tables (timestamps)</td></tr></table><p><b>Bitmap Scans (Slides 48–51 &amp; Exam Q1b-ii):</b> not a separate <code>CREATE INDEX</code> type in Postgres, but a <b>two-phase execution plan</b> chosen for moderately selective queries or when combining multiple indexes with bitwise <b>AND / OR</b>: (1) <b>Bitmap Index Scan</b> walks the B-tree/Hash index and builds an in-memory bitmap (1 bit per row/page); (2) <b>Bitmap Heap Scan</b> visits the table pages in sequential physical disk order (avoiding random I/O). Plan output shows <code>Heap Blocks: exact=N</code> (row-level bitmap fits in <code>work_mem</code>) vs <code>lossy=N</code> (bitmap degraded to page-level; Postgres must recheck rows on those pages). Audit unused indexes in <code>pg_stat_user_indexes</code>.</p>`}],
recall:[['Default PostgreSQL index type?','B-tree.'],['Which index type suits arrays and JSONB?','GIN.'],['What does EXPLAIN ANALYZE show?','The executed query plan with actual times and rows (e.g. Seq Scan vs Index Scan).'],['Cost of indexes?','Extra storage and slower INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE.'],['What is the time complexity of a sequential scan vs a B+-tree search with branching factor b?','Sequential scan: O(N). B+-tree search: O(log_b N). For branching factor 10 and 1,000,000 rows, a B+-tree needs only 6 lookups.'],['In a B+-tree of order (degree) n, how many keys and pointers can each node hold, and how do leaf nodes support fast range queries?','Each node holds up to n - 1 sorted keys and n pointers. Leaf nodes are chained left-to-right via their last pointer P_n, so a range query finds the starting leaf and walks horizontally across the leaves without climbing back up the tree.'],['Distinguish "What we index" from "How we index" in PostgreSQL (Slide 30).','What we index is the scope/purpose: single-column, composite, covering (INCLUDE), partial (WHERE), or functional/expression. How we index is the data structure: B-tree (default), Hash, GIN, GiST, SP-GiST, or BRIN.'],['What is a Covering Index in PostgreSQL, what SQL clause creates it, and what scan type does it unlock?','An index that stores extra payload columns alongside the search key using the INCLUDE clause (e.g. CREATE INDEX idx ON ratings(rating) INCLUDE (movie_id, votes)), enabling an Index Only Scan with Heap Fetches: 0.'],['What is a Partial Index in PostgreSQL and how is it created?','An index built over only a subset of rows matching a WHERE predicate, e.g. CREATE INDEX idx_blockbuster ON ratings(votes) WHERE votes > 100000;'],['Explain how PostgreSQL executes a two-phase Bitmap Scan and when it chooses one over a plain Index Scan.','Phase 1 (Bitmap Index Scan) scans one or more indexes to build an in-memory bitmap of matching row/page locations (and can combine multiple index bitmaps with bitwise AND/OR). Phase 2 (Bitmap Heap Scan) reads the matching table pages in physical disk order. Postgres chooses it for medium-selectivity queries or multi-column AND/OR filters where a plain Index Scan would cause too much random disk I/O.'],['In a Bitmap Heap Scan plan, what do "Heap Blocks: exact=N" and "lossy=N" mean, and which view tracks unused indexes?','exact=N means the bitmap stored exact row pointers for N pages; lossy=N means memory filled up so the bitmap only tracked the page number and Postgres had to recheck every row on those N pages. Unused indexes are checked in pg_stat_user_indexes.']],
quiz:[{k:'mcq',q:'Which query can use an index on LOWER(email)?',o:['WHERE email = \'x\'','WHERE LOWER(email) = \'x\'','WHERE UPPER(email) = \'X\'','WHERE email LIKE \'%x\''],a:1},{k:'gap',q:'Without a usable index, PostgreSQL does a ___ scan.',a:'seq',alt:['sequential']},{k:'tf',q:'Indexes speed up both reads and writes.',a:false},{k:'mcq',q:'In a B+-tree of degree (order) n, what is the maximum number of search keys and pointers a single node can hold?',o:['n keys and n + 1 pointers','n - 1 keys and n pointers','2n keys and 2n pointers','1 key and 2 pointers always'],a:1,w:undefined},{k:'mcq',q:'Why are leaf nodes in a B+-tree chained together left-to-right by their final pointer P_n?',o:['To slow down insertions','So range queries can scan consecutive sorted keys across leaves without climbing back up the tree','To compute SHA-256 hashes','Because B+-trees have no root node'],a:1,w:undefined},{k:'mcq',q:'Which PostgreSQL index access method has O(1) lookup complexity and supports ONLY the equality (=) operator?',o:['B-tree','Hash (USING hash)','BRIN','GiST'],a:1,w:undefined},{k:'mcq',q:'Which SQL clause adds non-key payload columns to a B-tree index so PostgreSQL can perform an Index Only Scan (Heap Fetches: 0)?',o:['WHERE','INCLUDE','RETURNING','UNNEST'],a:1,w:undefined},{k:'mcq',q:'CREATE INDEX idx_blockbuster ON ratings(votes) WHERE votes > 100000; creates which kind of index?',o:['A partial (filtered) index','A hash index','A functional index','A full-text GIN index'],a:0,w:undefined},{k:'mcq',q:'In an EXPLAIN ANALYZE plan, when does PostgreSQL combine a Bitmap Index Scan with a Bitmap Heap Scan?',o:['Only when no indexes exist on the table','For moderately selective queries or when combining multiple indexes with bitwise AND/OR to read table pages in physical order','Only for INSERT statements','When executing a ROLLBACK'],a:1,w:undefined},{k:'gap',q:'In a Bitmap Heap Scan, when the bitmap only remembers the page rather than exact rows, the Heap Blocks line reports ___ pages.',a:'lossy',w:undefined}],
theory:[{q:'Write SQL to (a) create a B-tree index on customers.email, (b) check whether it is used, (c) create a case-insensitive index and a query that uses it.',pts:['CREATE INDEX','EXPLAIN ANALYZE','Seq vs Index scan','LOWER expression index','Matching query'],model:`<pre>CREATE INDEX idx_email ON customers(email);
EXPLAIN ANALYZE SELECT * FROM customers WHERE email = 'ada@pau.edu.ng';
CREATE INDEX idx_email_lower ON customers(LOWER(email));
SELECT * FROM customers WHERE LOWER(email) = LOWER('Ada@PAU.edu.ng');</pre>`},{q:'(Feb 2026 Exam Q1b & Q1c) (a) Write SQL to create a B+-tree index idx_instructor_name on instructor(name). (b) Explain how a B+-tree improves query performance and discuss one scenario where the query engine uses a bitmap index scan alongside it. (c) Simulate inserting \'Wu\', \'Einstein\', \'Katz\', \'Singh\', \'Alimi\', \'Crick\', \'Brandt\', \'Gold\' into a B+-tree of degree 3, showing every split, and explain how a range query BETWEEN \'Alimi\' AND \'Gold\' uses the final tree.',pts:['CREATE INDEX idx_instructor_name ON instructor(name);','B+-tree performance: O(log_b N) balanced sorted tree vs O(N) Seq Scan + automatic local self-reorganisation','Bitmap scan scenario: medium selectivity or combining idx_instructor_name with another index (e.g. deptname = \'Comp. Sci.\' OR salary > 100000) via bitwise AND/OR in a Bitmap Index Scan + sequential physical I/O in Bitmap Heap Scan','Degree 3 B+-tree rule: max 2 keys, 3 pointers per node; split on 3rd key','All 5 splits traced accurately to final 3-level tree (Root [Katz] -> [Crick|Einstein], [Singh] -> 5 chained leaves)','Range query BETWEEN \'Alimi\' AND \'Gold\': root -> left leaf [Alimi|Brandt] -> follow horizontal P_3 leaf pointers through [Crick] to [Einstein|Gold]'],model:`<p><b>Background:</b> without an index, PostgreSQL executes a sequential scan of $O(N)$ complexity, inspecting every row in <code>instructor</code>. Creating a B+-tree index organises search keys into a self-balancing multilevel tree of $O(\\log_b N)$ depth with horizontally linked leaf nodes.</p><p><b>Answer and solution:</b></p><ul><li><b>1. SQL Command (Q1b-i):</b><pre>CREATE INDEX idx_instructor_name ON instructor (name);</pre></li><li><b>2. How B+-Trees Improve Performance &amp; Bitmap Scan Scenario (Q1b-ii):</b> a B+-tree keeps keys sorted in internal router nodes and leaf nodes, cutting lookups from $O(N)$ to $O(\\log_b N)$ and maintaining balance via small local node splits without full-file reorganisation. <b>Bitmap scan scenario:</b> if a query filters on a moderately selective condition (returning many rows across scattered disk pages) or combines <code>idx_instructor_name</code> with a second index—for example, <code>WHERE name LIKE 'A%' OR deptname = 'Comp. Sci.'</code>—PostgreSQL runs a <b>Bitmap Index Scan</b> on each B+-tree to build in-memory bitmaps of matching row locations, combines them with a bitwise <b>OR/AND</b>, and then runs a <b>Bitmap Heap Scan</b> to read the surviving table pages in physical disk order, avoiding random I/O thrashing.</li><li><b>3. Degree-3 B+-Tree Simulation (Q1c-i — max $n-1=2$ keys and $n=3$ pointers per node):</b><ol><li><b>Insert 'Wu', then 'Einstein':</b> Leaf: <code>[Einstein | Wu]</code>.</li><li><b>Insert 'Katz' (Split 1 — leaf overflow at 3 keys):</b> <code>{Einstein, Katz, Wu}</code> splits into <code>[Einstein]</code> and <code>[Katz | Wu]</code>; middle key <code>'Katz'</code> is promoted to root: Root <code>[Katz]</code>.</li><li><b>Insert 'Singh' (Split 2 — right leaf overflow):</b> <code>{Katz, Singh, Wu}</code> splits into <code>[Katz]</code> and <code>[Singh | Wu]</code>; <code>'Singh'</code> is promoted to root: Root <code>[Katz | Singh]</code> → Leaves: <code>[Einstein] → [Katz] → [Singh | Wu]</code>.</li><li><b>Insert 'Alimi':</b> fits in leftmost leaf: <code>[Alimi | Einstein]</code>.</li><li><b>Insert 'Crick' (Split 3 — leftmost leaf overflow &amp; Split 4 — root overflow):</b> <code>{Alimi, Crick, Einstein}</code> splits into <code>[Alimi]</code> and <code>[Crick | Einstein]</code>, promoting <code>'Crick'</code>. The root now overflows with <code>{Crick, Katz, Singh}</code> and splits internally: middle key <code>'Katz'</code> becomes the new top root <code>[Katz]</code>, with left internal child <code>[Crick]</code> and right internal child <code>[Singh]</code>.</li><li><b>Insert 'Brandt':</b> fits in leftmost leaf: <code>[Alimi | Brandt]</code>.</li><li><b>Insert 'Gold' (Split 5 — leaf [Crick | Einstein] overflow):</b> <code>{Crick, Einstein, Gold}</code> splits into <code>[Crick]</code> and <code>[Einstein | Gold]</code>, promoting <code>'Einstein'</code> into the left internal node <code>[Crick | Einstein]</code> (2 keys, fits without splitting).</li></ol><pre>                    [     Katz     ]                 &lt;-- Top Root
                   /                \\
        [ Crick | Einstein ]      [ Singh ]          &lt;-- Internal Nodes
        /        |         \\       /      \\
[Alimi|Brandt]→[Crick]→[Einstein|Gold]→[Katz]→[Singh|Wu]  &lt;-- Linked Leaves</pre></li><li><b>4. Range Query <code>BETWEEN 'Alimi' AND 'Gold'</code> (Q1c-ii):</b> Postgres starts at root <code>[Katz]</code> (<code>'Alimi' &lt; 'Katz'</code> → go left to <code>[Crick | Einstein]</code>; <code>'Alimi' &lt; 'Crick'</code> → descend to leftmost leaf <code>[Alimi | Brandt]</code>). Once at <code>'Alimi'</code>, it reads <code>'Alimi'</code> and <code>'Brandt'</code>, follows the leaf's horizontal next-pointer $P_3$ to <code>[Crick]</code>, then follows $P_3$ to <code>[Einstein | Gold]</code>, reads <code>'Einstein'</code> and <code>'Gold'</code>, and stops—never climbing back up the internal tree.</li></ul><p><b>Scenario &amp; Conclusion:</b> logarithmic tree descent locates the start of any range in 3 hops, while the horizontal leaf chain delivers sequential scan speed across the matching range.</p>`}]},
{id:'t3',title:'Complex types: arrays and JSON/JSONB',why:'Array queries (ANY, cardinality, array_append/remove, unnest) and JSONB operators.',
cards:[
{h:'Arrays',b:`<pre>CREATE TABLE products(id serial, name text, tags text[]);
INSERT INTO products(name, tags) VALUES ('Lamp', ARRAY['home','light']);
SELECT * FROM products WHERE 'home' = ANY(tags);
SELECT name, cardinality(tags) FROM products;
UPDATE products SET tags = array_append(tags, 'sale') WHERE id = 1;
UPDATE products SET tags = array_remove(tags, 'light') WHERE id = 1;
SELECT name, unnest(tags) AS tag FROM products;
SELECT * FROM products WHERE tags @&gt; ARRAY['home'];</pre><p>Arrays are <b>1-indexed</b>: <code>tags[1]</code>.</p>`},
{h:'JSON/JSONB',b:`<p><b>JSONB</b> = binary, indexable (GIN), no duplicate keys; <b>JSON</b> = stored as text.</p><pre>SELECT data-&gt;'address'-&gt;&gt;'city' FROM customers;
SELECT * FROM customers WHERE data @&gt; '{"vip": true}';
SELECT * FROM customers WHERE data ? 'phone';
UPDATE customers SET data = jsonb_set(data, '{vip}', 'true');</pre><p><code>-&gt;</code> returns JSON; <code>-&gt;&gt;</code> returns text.</p>`},
{h:'Composite types (CREATE TYPE) & field notation (Exam Q2a–b)',b:`<p>In PostgreSQL, a <b>user-defined composite type</b> groups structured fields (such as <code>name, email, phone, address</code>) into a single reusable column type for consistency across tables (<b>Exam Q2a–b</b> &amp; <b>Q5 Figure 3</b>):</p><pre>-- 1. Create the composite type and table (Exam Q2a-i)
CREATE TYPE contact_info AS (
  name TEXT,
  email TEXT,
  phone TEXT,
  address TEXT
);
CREATE TABLE users (
  user_id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
  profile contact_info NOT NULL,
  preferences TEXT[] DEFAULT '{}'
);

-- 2. Insert a composite value using ROW(...) or '(...)' literal (Exam Q2b-i)
INSERT INTO users (profile)
VALUES (ROW('Dele Giwa', 'dele_giwa@gmail.com', '+2348123226745', 'Ikeja, Lagos'));

-- 3. Update ONLY one subfield of a composite column (Exam Q2b-ii)
-- TRAP: do NOT write profile.phone on the left of SET; writeprofile.phone without parentheses!
UPDATE users
SET profile.phone = '+2348012345678'
WHERE user_id = 1;

-- 4. Read/filter a subfield of a composite column (Exam Q2b-iii)
-- TRAP: wrap the column name in parentheses: (profile).address
DELETE FROM users
WHERE (profile).address = 'Test Location';</pre><div class="trap"><b>Without the array type (Exam Q2a-ii):</b> if PostgreSQL had no array type for <code>preferences</code>, a normalized relational design would require creating a separate <b><code>user_preferences(user_id INT REFERENCES users, preference TEXT, PRIMARY KEY (user_id, preference))</code></b> table (or a lookup table + junction table) and joining it on every read.</div>`},
{h:'Complete array & JSONB operator toolkit (Lecture 2 & CA1)',b:`<table><tr><th>Array Feature</th><th>Syntax &amp; Meaning</th></tr><tr><td>Literals &amp; 1-indexing</td><td><code>'{a,b}'</code> or <code>ARRAY['a','b']</code> · <code>mobiles[1]</code> (1st) · slice <code>mobiles[1:2]</code> · empty <code>'{}'</code> $\\ne$ <code>NULL</code></td></tr><tr><td>Search &amp; compare</td><td><code>'val' = ANY(arr)</code> · containment <code>arr @&gt; ARRAY['a','b']</code> · contained by <code>arr &lt;@ ...</code> · overlap <code>arr &amp;&amp; ARRAY['a','c']</code> (shares $\\ge 1$)</td></tr><tr><td>Functions</td><td><code>cardinality(arr)</code> · <code>array_append(arr, v)</code> · <code>array_remove(arr, v)</code> · <code>array_cat(arr1, arr2)</code> · <code>array_position(arr, v)</code> · <code>unnest(arr)</code> (rows) ↔ <code>array_agg(x)</code> (back to array)</td></tr></table><table><tr><th>JSONB Feature</th><th>Syntax &amp; Meaning</th></tr><tr><td>Extract &amp; cast</td><td><code>-&gt;</code> returns JSONB (0-indexed for arrays: <code>items -&gt; 0 -&gt;&gt; 'product_id'</code>) · <code>-&gt;&gt;</code> returns TEXT (cast numbers: <code>(v.type_details -&gt;&gt; 'weight_kg')::numeric &gt; 5</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>Test keys &amp; fragments</td><td><code>doc ? 'key'</code> (top-level key exists) · <code>doc ?&amp; ARRAY['k1','k2']</code> (all keys exist) · <code>doc ?| ARRAY['k1','k2']</code> (any key exists) · <code>doc @&gt; '{"battery": "5000mAh"}'</code> (containment)</td></tr><tr><td>Expand &amp; modify</td><td><code>jsonb_array_elements(items)</code> · <code>jsonb_set(doc, '{path}', '"val"')</code> (or <code>'{1,quantity}', '3'</code>) · remove top key: <code>doc - 'key'</code> · remove nested path: <code>doc #- '{0}'</code></td></tr><tr><td>GIN indexing</td><td><code>CREATE INDEX idx ON products USING GIN (tags);</code> (speeds up <code>@&gt;, &amp;&amp;, ?</code>)</td></tr></table><p><b>When to use complex types (Lecture 2 Slide 5):</b> use arrays/JSONB when data is static/rarely updated, sparse or flexible in shape; use separate relational tables when data is updated often, joined, aggregated or needs foreign-key enforcement (foreign keys cannot point inside arrays or JSONB).</p>`}],
recall:[['-> vs ->>?','-> returns JSON; ->> returns text.'],['How do you find rows where an array contains a value?','WHERE \'value\' = ANY(col) or col @> ARRAY[\'value\'].'],['What does unnest do?','Expands an array into a set of rows.'],['JSON vs JSONB?','JSONB is binary, indexable and faster to query; JSON keeps the raw text.'],['Are PostgreSQL arrays 0- or 1-indexed?','1-indexed.'],['How do you create a composite type contact_info(name, email, phone, address) in PostgreSQL, and how do you read vs update one of its subfields?','CREATE TYPE contact_info AS (name TEXT, email TEXT, phone TEXT, address TEXT); To read/filter a subfield in SELECT/WHERE, put parentheses around the column: (profile).address = \'Test Location\'. To update one subfield in SET, omit parentheses: SET profile.phone = \'+2348012345678\'.'],['In PostgreSQL arrays, what is the difference between @> (containment) and && (overlap)?','a @> b is true when array a contains every element of array b; a && b is true when the two arrays share at least one element in common.'],['How do you remove "Groceries" and append "Laptops" to an interests TEXT[] column in a single SQL UPDATE statement (CA1 Q6-1)?','UPDATE customers SET interests = array_append(array_remove(interests, \'Groceries\'), \'Laptops\') WHERE account_no = \'CUS/2023/00002\'; (or using array_cat(array_remove(interests, \'Groceries\'), \'{Laptops}\')).'],['Why are SQL arrays 1-indexed while JSONB arrays accessed with -> are 0-indexed?','PostgreSQL SQL arrays follow the SQL standard and start at subscript [1] (e.g. mobiles[1]), whereas JSON arrays follow JavaScript/JSON convention and start at 0 (e.g. items -> 0 ->> \'product_id\').'],['How do you remove a top-level key vs a nested path from a JSONB column in PostgreSQL?','Top-level key: type_details - \'shipping_class\'. Nested path or array element: items #- \'{0}\' or doc #- \'{a,b}\'.'],['What is one major advantage of storing multi-valued categories in a PostgreSQL array over a normalized design, and one major advantage of JSONB for product specs (Exam Q2c-ii & Q2d-iii)?','Arrays eliminate the extra junction table and expensive multi-table JOINs when reading a product\'s categories together. JSONB allows sparse, varying product attributes (battery for phones, sizes for shirts, file_size for eBooks) in one column without ALTER TABLE migrations or dozens of NULL columns.']],
quiz:[{k:'mcq',q:'Number of elements in tags?',o:['length(tags)','cardinality(tags)','count(tags)','size(tags)'],a:1},{k:'gap',q:'The JSONB operator that tests if a key exists is ___.',a:'?'},{k:'tf',q:'array_remove(tags, \'x\') removes all occurrences of x.',a:true},{k:'mcq',q:'Given column profile of composite type contact_info, which WHERE clause correctly filters by the address subfield in PostgreSQL?',o:['WHERE profile.address = \'Test Location\'','WHERE (profile).address = \'Test Location\'','WHERE profile->\'address\' = \'Test Location\'','WHERE address(profile) = \'Test Location\''],a:1,w:undefined},{k:'mcq',q:'Given column profile of composite type contact_info, which SET clause updates ONLY the phone subfield for user_id = 1?',o:['SET (profile).phone = \'+2348012345678\'','SET profile.phone = \'+2348012345678\'','SET profile = jsonb_set(profile, \'{phone}\', \'+234\')','SET phone = \'+2348012345678\''],a:1,w:undefined},{k:'mcq',q:'Which PostgreSQL array operator checks whether two arrays share AT LEAST ONE element in common (overlap)?',o:['@>','<@','&&','->>'],a:2,w:undefined},{k:'mcq',q:'In PostgreSQL, what index does items -> 0 ->> \'product_id\' access inside a JSONB array?',o:['The first element (JSONB arrays are 0-indexed)','The second element','An error because JSONB arrays start at 1','The last element'],a:0,w:undefined},{k:'mcq',q:'Which expression removes the top-level key \'shipping_class\' from a JSONB column named type_details?',o:['type_details - \'shipping_class\'','array_remove(type_details, \'shipping_class\')','type_details ? \'shipping_class\'','unnest(type_details)'],a:0,w:undefined},{k:'mcq',q:'Which PostgreSQL function expands a JSONB array of objects into one row per JSON element (the JSON equivalent of unnest)?',o:['jsonb_set()','jsonb_array_elements()','jsonb_pretty()','array_agg()'],a:1,w:undefined}],
theory:[{q:'business_db: suppliers(id, name, delivery_days int[]). Write queries to (a) list suppliers delivering on day 3, (b) count delivery days per supplier, (c) add day 6 to supplier 2, (d) total deliveries across all suppliers.',pts:['ANY','cardinality','array_append','unnest or SUM(cardinality)','Correct syntax'],model:`<pre>SELECT name FROM suppliers WHERE 3 = ANY(delivery_days);
SELECT name, cardinality(delivery_days) FROM suppliers;
UPDATE suppliers SET delivery_days = array_append(delivery_days, 6) WHERE id = 2;
SELECT SUM(cardinality(delivery_days)) FROM suppliers;
-- or: SELECT COUNT(*) FROM suppliers, unnest(delivery_days);</pre>`},{q:'(Feb 2026 Exam Q2 Complete) Solve Case Study 2 (Online Marketplace for Electronics): (a) Write SQL to create composite type contact_info(name, email, phone, address) and table users(user_id SERIAL PK, profile contact_info NOT NULL, preferences TEXT[] DEFAULT \'{}\'), and explain how preferences would be modelled without arrays. (b) Insert Dele Giwa (user_id 1), update only his phone number, and delete all users at \'Test Location\'. (c) Insert product \'Samsung Galaxy\' with categories [\'smartphone\',\'android\',\'wireless\'] and state one advantage of arrays over 1NF tables. (d) Update product_id=13 specs (JSONB) with {"battery":"5000mAh","screen":"6.5 inches","colour":"black"}, query products containing {"battery":"5000mAh"}, and explain how JSONB solves rigid-schema problems.',pts:['CREATE TYPE contact_info AS (...) + CREATE TABLE users (...)','Without array type: separate user_preferences(user_id FK, preference) child/junction table + JOINs','INSERT INTO users (profile) VALUES (ROW(\'Dele Giwa\', ...))','UPDATE users SET profile.phone = \'+2348012345678\' WHERE user_id = 1;','DELETE FROM users WHERE (profile).address = \'Test Location\';','INSERT INTO products (name, categories) VALUES (\'Samsung Galaxy\', ARRAY[\'smartphone\',\'android\',\'wireless\']) + array advantage (fewer tables, no JOIN overhead)','UPDATE products SET specs = \'{"battery":"5000mAh","screen":"6.5 inches","colour":"black"}\'::jsonb WHERE product_id = 13;','SELECT * FROM products WHERE specs @> \'{"battery": "5000mAh"}\'; + JSONB schema-flexibility explanation'],model:`<p><b>Background:</b> PostgreSQL's complex types—user-defined composite types, arrays and JSONB—let a relational database model structured contact details, multi-valued lists and sparse product specifications without table sprawl while keeping SQL constraints and GIN indexing.</p><p><b>Answer and solution (Complete SQL &amp; Explanations for Exam Q2):</b></p><ul><li><b>(a-i) Create Composite Type &amp; Users Table (5.5 marks):</b><pre>CREATE TYPE contact_info AS (
    name    TEXT,
    email   TEXT,
    phone   TEXT,
    address TEXT
);

CREATE TABLE users (
    user_id     SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    profile     contact_info NOT NULL,
    preferences TEXT[] DEFAULT '{}'
);</pre></li><li><b>(a-ii) Modelling <code>preferences</code> if the Array Type Did Not Exist (2.5 marks):</b> to satisfy First Normal Form (1NF), the designer would remove <code>preferences</code> from <code>users</code> and create a separate child table <code>user_preferences(user_id INT REFERENCES users(user_id) ON DELETE CASCADE, preference TEXT, PRIMARY KEY (user_id, preference))</code> (or a <code>preferences</code> lookup table plus a many-to-many junction table), storing one row per user preference and joining on <code>user_id</code> when querying.</li><li><b>(b-i) Insert First User Dele Giwa (2.5 marks):</b><pre>INSERT INTO users (profile)
VALUES (ROW('Dele Giwa', 'dele_giwa@gmail.com', '+2348123226745', 'Ikeja, Lagos'));</pre></li><li><b>(b-ii) Update Only the Phone Subfield for <code>user_id = 1</code> (2.5 marks):</b><pre>UPDATE users
SET profile.phone = '+2348012345678'
WHERE user_id = 1;</pre></li><li><b>(b-iii) Delete All Dummy Users at <code>'Test Location'</code> (2 marks):</b><pre>DELETE FROM users
WHERE (profile).address = 'Test Location';</pre></li><li><b>(c-i) Insert <code>'Samsung Galaxy'</code> with Array Categories (2.5 marks):</b><pre>INSERT INTO products (name, categories)
VALUES ('Samsung Galaxy', ARRAY['smartphone', 'android', 'wireless']);</pre></li><li><b>(c-ii) Major Advantage of Arrays Over Normalized SQL (2.5 marks):</b> storing categories in a <code>TEXT[]</code> column keeps all categories inside the product row, eliminating the extra junction table and avoiding costly multi-table <code>JOIN</code> operations whenever a product and its categories are fetched together.</li><li><b>(d-i) Add JSONB Specs to <code>'Samsung Galaxy A17'</code> (<code>product_id = 13</code>) (1.5 marks):</b><pre>UPDATE products
SET specs = '{"battery": "5000mAh", "screen": "6.5 inches", "colour": "black"}'::jsonb
WHERE product_id = 13;</pre></li><li><b>(d-ii) Query Products Whose <code>specs</code> Contain <code>{"battery": "5000mAh"}</code> (1.5 marks):</b><pre>SELECT * FROM products
WHERE specs @&gt; '{"battery": "5000mAh"}';
-- (or: WHERE specs -&gt;&gt; 'battery' = '5000mAh')</pre></li><li><b>(d-iii) How JSONB Alleviates Rigid-Schema Problems (2 marks):</b> in an electronics marketplace, smartphones have <code>battery</code> and <code>screen</code>, laptops have <code>CPU</code> and <code>RAM</code>, and cables have <code>length</code>. A rigid relational schema would either need dozens of sparse <code>NULL</code> columns in one wide table or a separate subtype table per product category; <code>JSONB</code> lets every product store its own custom attributes in a single binary-indexed column without running <code>ALTER TABLE</code> whenever a new gadget type arrives.</li></ul><p><b>Scenario &amp; Conclusion:</b> combining a relational primary key (<code>user_id</code>, <code>product_id</code>) with a composite <code>contact_info</code>, a <code>TEXT[]</code> array and a <code>JSONB</code> column delivers the exact hybrid architecture recommended in Lecture 2.</p>`}]},
{id:'t4',title:'Functions, procedures and triggers',why:'PL/pgSQL syntax: write a function, a procedure and a trigger.',
cards:[
{h:'Functions and procedures',b:`<pre>CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION order_total(p_id int)
RETURNS numeric AS $$
BEGIN
  RETURN (SELECT SUM(qty*price) FROM order_items WHERE order_id = p_id);
END; $$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;
SELECT order_total(10);

CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE restock(p_id int, n int)
LANGUAGE plpgsql AS $$
BEGIN
  UPDATE products SET stock = stock + n WHERE id = p_id;
  COMMIT;
END; $$;
CALL restock(4, 20);</pre><p>Function: returns a value, used in SELECT. Procedure: invoked with CALL, can control transactions.</p>`},
{h:'Triggers',b:`<pre>CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION log_price() RETURNS trigger AS $$
BEGIN
  INSERT INTO price_log(product_id, old_price, new_price, changed_at)
  VALUES (OLD.id, OLD.price, NEW.price, now());
  RETURN NEW;
END; $$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;

CREATE TRIGGER trg_price
AFTER UPDATE OF price ON products
FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION log_price();</pre><p><b>NEW</b>/<b>OLD</b> rows; BEFORE vs AFTER; FOR EACH ROW vs FOR EACH STATEMENT.</p>`},
{h:'SQL Functions vs PL/pgSQL Functions',b:`<p>PostgreSQL supports two main languages for server-side functions:</p><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th><code>LANGUAGE sql</code></th><th><code>LANGUAGE plpgsql</code></th></tr><tr><td>Body structure</td><td>Pure SQL statements (<code>SELECT</code>, <code>INSERT</code>, etc.)</td><td>Block-structured procedural code (<code>DECLARE ... BEGIN ... END;</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>Variables &amp; loops</td><td>Not supported</td><td>Variables (<code>:=</code>), <code>IF/THEN/ELSE</code>, <code>FOR/WHILE</code> loops</td></tr><tr><td>Error handling</td><td>Standard SQL errors only</td><td><code>FOUND</code> flag, <code>RAISE NOTICE</code>, <code>RAISE EXCEPTION</code></td></tr><tr><td>Best for</td><td>Simple single-expression calculations or table queries</td><td>Multi-step business logic, validation, and dynamic computation</td></tr></table>`},
{h:'PL/pgSQL Anatomy: DECLARE, INTO, FOUND & RAISE',b:`<p>Inside a <code>LANGUAGE plpgsql</code> block:</p><ul><li><b><code>DECLARE</code></b> defines local variables and assigns defaults with <code>:=</code> (or <code>=</code>).</li><li><b><code>SELECT col INTO var</code></b> stores a query result into a local variable.</li><li><b><code>IF NOT FOUND THEN</code></b> checks the special boolean <code>FOUND</code>, which is <code>false</code> when <code>SELECT ... INTO</code> returns zero rows.</li><li><b><code>RAISE EXCEPTION 'msg', val;</code></b> aborts the current transaction and rolls back changes; <b><code>RAISE NOTICE</code></b> prints an informational message without aborting.</li></ul><pre>CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION stock_status(p_prod_id INT, p_var_id INT)
RETURNS TEXT AS $$
DECLARE
  v_qty INT;
BEGIN
  SELECT qty_on_hand INTO v_qty FROM variations
  WHERE product_id = p_prod_id AND variation_id = p_var_id;
  IF NOT FOUND THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'Variation (%, %) not found', p_prod_id, p_var_id;
  END IF;
  IF v_qty = 0 THEN RETURN 'Out of stock';
  ELSIF v_qty &lt; 20 THEN RETURN 'Low stock (' || v_qty || ' left)';
  ELSE RETURN 'In stock (' || v_qty || ' available)';
  END IF;
END; $$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;</pre>`},
{h:'Table-Returning Functions: RETURNS TABLE & RETURN QUERY',b:`<p>When a function returns multiple rows and columns, declare <code>RETURNS TABLE(col1 type1, ...)</code> and use <code>RETURN QUERY</code> in PL/pgSQL (or a plain <code>SELECT</code> in <code>LANGUAGE sql</code>). Call it in the <code>FROM</code> clause like a table:</p><pre>CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION customer_orders(p_user_id INT)
RETURNS TABLE(order_id TEXT, created_at TIMESTAMPTZ, total INT, status TEXT) AS $$
BEGIN
  RETURN QUERY
  SELECT o.order_id, o.created_at, o.total, o.status
  FROM orders o WHERE o.user_id = p_user_id
  ORDER BY o.created_at DESC;
END; $$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;

-- Usage:
SELECT * FROM customer_orders(3);</pre><div class="tip"><b>Column name collision rule:</b> Prefix table columns with the table alias (e.g. <code>o.order_id</code>) so PostgreSQL does not confuse them with the output parameter names declared in <code>RETURNS TABLE(...)</code>.</div>`},
{h:'Functions vs Stored Procedures (Exam Q3a)',b:`<p>Introduced in PostgreSQL 11, <b>stored procedures</b> differ from functions in five exam-critical ways:</p><table><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Function (<code>CREATE FUNCTION</code>)</th><th>Procedure (<code>CREATE PROCEDURE</code>)</th></tr><tr><td>Invocation</td><td>Inside a SQL expression: <code>SELECT fn(x);</code> or <code>WHERE fn(x) = ...</code></td><td>Standalone command: <code>CALL proc(x);</code> (cannot be used inside <code>SELECT</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>Return value</td><td>Must declare <code>RETURNS type</code> (or <code>RETURNS VOID</code> / <code>TABLE</code>)</td><td>Does not return a value (can use <code>INOUT</code> parameters if needed)</td></tr><tr><td>Transaction control</td><td>Runs inside the caller's transaction; <b>cannot</b> execute <code>COMMIT</code> or <code>ROLLBACK</code></td><td>Can manage transactions directly with <code>COMMIT</code> and <code>ROLLBACK</code></td></tr><tr><td>Primary purpose</td><td>Compute and return a value or result set without side effects</td><td>Execute multi-step write workflows across multiple tables</td></tr></table>`},
{h:'Splendid Stores Stored Procedure: CALL make_order',b:`<p>In Lecture 3, <code>make_order(p_user_id INT, items JSONB)</code> places a complete customer order atomically:</p><ol><li>Loops through the JSONB items array using <code>FOR item IN SELECT jsonb_array_elements(items) LOOP</code>.</li><li>Deducts stock and fetches the live price in one statement: <code>UPDATE variations SET qty_on_hand = qty_on_hand - quantity WHERE ... RETURNING price INTO STRICT item_price;</code>.</li><li>Injects <code>unit_price</code> into each JSONB array element using <code>jsonb_set(items, ARRAY[counter::text, 'unit_price'], to_jsonb(item_price), true)</code>.</li><li>Inserts the new row into <code>orders</code> using <code>create_order_id(p_user_id)</code> and <code>compute_order_total(items)</code>.</li></ol><div class="tip"><b>Atomicity on failure:</b> If item #2 triggers the <code>qty_on_hand &gt;= 0</code> <code>CHECK</code> constraint, the entire <code>CALL make_order(...)</code> rolls back, automatically restoring the stock deducted for item #1!</div>`},
{h:'Triggers: BEFORE vs AFTER, ROW vs STATEMENT',b:`<p>A PostgreSQL trigger pairs a <b>trigger function</b> (<code>RETURNS TRIGGER</code>) with a <b><code>CREATE TRIGGER</code></b> attachment on a table.</p><table><tr><th>Option</th><th>Behaviour &amp; Special Variables</th></tr><tr><td><b><code>BEFORE</code></b></td><td>Fires before the row is written. Can modify <code>NEW.col := val</code> to auto-fill columns (like <code>NEW.total</code>) or <code>RETURN NULL</code> to silently cancel the operation on that row.</td></tr><tr><td><b><code>AFTER</code></b></td><td>Fires after the write succeeds. Cannot alter <code>NEW</code>; used for audit logging or updating related summary tables.</td></tr><tr><td><b><code>FOR EACH ROW</code></b></td><td>Fires once per affected row. Provides <code>NEW</code> (for <code>INSERT</code>/<code>UPDATE</code>) and <code>OLD</code> (for <code>UPDATE</code>/<code>DELETE</code>).</td></tr><tr><td><b><code>FOR EACH STATEMENT</code></b></td><td>Fires once per SQL statement regardless of how many rows changed (even if 0 rows matched).</td></tr><tr><td><b><code>TG_OP</code> / <code>WHEN</code></b></td><td><code>TG_OP</code> holds <code>'INSERT'</code>, <code>'UPDATE'</code>, or <code>'DELETE'</code>; <code>WHEN (OLD.items IS DISTINCT FROM NEW.items)</code> skips firing unless specific columns changed.</td></tr></table>`},
{h:'Choosing the Right Tool: Constraint vs Function vs Procedure vs Trigger',b:`<table><tr><th>Requirement</th><th>Best PostgreSQL Mechanism</th></tr><tr><td>Single-row invariant (e.g., <code>qty_on_hand &gt;= 0</code>, <code>price &gt; 0</code>)</td><td><b><code>CHECK</code> Constraint</b> (declarative, fastest, cannot be bypassed)</td></tr><tr><td>Reusable calculation or filtered view inside queries</td><td><b>Function</b> (<code>CREATE FUNCTION</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>Explicit multi-table business workflow invoked by the app</td><td><b>Stored Procedure</b> (<code>CALL make_order(...)</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>Automatic rule that must run on every write regardless of which app or user issues the query</td><td><b>Trigger</b> (<code>BEFORE</code> to validate/mutate <code>NEW</code>; <code>AFTER</code> to audit/cascade)</td></tr></table>`}],
recall:[['Function vs procedure in PostgreSQL?','A function returns a value and is called in queries; a procedure is run with CALL and can COMMIT/ROLLBACK.'],['What must a trigger function return?','The trigger type (RETURNS trigger), usually RETURN NEW (or OLD/NULL).'],['NEW vs OLD?','NEW is the row after the change; OLD is the row before.'],['What are the four key differences between a PostgreSQL function and a stored procedure (Exam Q3a)?','1) Invocation: Functions are called inside SQL expressions (SELECT fn()), whereas procedures are invoked with CALL proc(). 2) Return value: Functions must specify a RETURNS clause, whereas procedures do not return a value. 3) Transaction control: Procedures can execute COMMIT and ROLLBACK inside their body, whereas functions cannot. 4) Purpose: Functions compute values/tables for queries, whereas procedures orchestrate multi-step write workflows.'],['In PL/pgSQL, what does the special variable FOUND indicate after a SELECT ... INTO statement, and how do you abort execution with an error?','FOUND is a boolean set to true if SELECT ... INTO returned at least one row, and false if no row matched. You abort execution and roll back the current transaction using RAISE EXCEPTION \'message\', arg;'],['How do you declare and return rows from a PL/pgSQL function that outputs a table of results?','Declare RETURNS TABLE(col1 type1, col2 type2, ...) in the function header, and use RETURN QUERY SELECT ... inside the BEGIN ... END block. Call the function in the FROM clause: SELECT * FROM fn(arg);'],['In the Lecture 3 make_order procedure, how does UPDATE ... RETURNING price INTO STRICT item_price work?','It decrements variations.qty_on_hand and simultaneously retrieves the row\'s price into item_price. STRICT ensures that if zero (or more than one) variation row matches, PostgreSQL immediately raises an exception and rolls back the order.'],['What happens in a BEFORE FOR EACH ROW trigger if the trigger function executes RETURN NULL instead of RETURN NEW?','PostgreSQL silently skips the INSERT, UPDATE, or DELETE for that specific row without raising an error, while continuing with any remaining rows.'],['Which special trigger record variables are available during INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE row-level triggers?','INSERT provides NEW only; DELETE provides OLD only; UPDATE provides both OLD (values before the update) and NEW (values after the update). TG_OP holds the operation string (\'INSERT\', \'UPDATE\', or \'DELETE\').'],['In Lecture 3, how does the BEFORE INSERT OR UPDATE trigger trg_order_total keep orders.total accurate?','When a row is inserted or when WHEN (OLD.items IS DISTINCT FROM NEW.items) is true on an update, the trigger function sets NEW.total := compute_order_total(NEW.items) and returns NEW before the row is written to disk.'],['How do you validate in a BEFORE trigger on products that every tag in NEW.tags exists in the allowed_tags table?','Check whether NEW.tags is contained in the array of valid tags: IF NOT (NEW.tags <@ ARRAY(SELECT tag FROM allowed_tags)) THEN RAISE EXCEPTION \'Unknown tag in %\', NEW.tags; END IF;']],
quiz:[{k:'mcq',q:'How do you run a procedure?',o:['SELECT proc()','CALL proc()','EXEC proc','RUN proc()'],a:1},{k:'gap',q:'A trigger function is declared RETURNS ___.',a:'trigger'},{k:'tf',q:'OLD is available in INSERT triggers.',a:false},{q:'Which statement correctly invokes a PostgreSQL stored procedure named `make_orders`?',o:['SELECT make_orders(3, \'[...]\'::jsonb);','CALL make_orders(3, \'[...]\'::jsonb);','EXEC PROCEDURE make_orders(3, \'[...]\'::jsonb);','PERFORM make_orders(3, \'[...]\'::jsonb);'],a:1,e:'Stored procedures in PostgreSQL are invoked with the standalone `CALL procedure_name(...);` statement, not `SELECT`.',k:'mcq',w:'Stored procedures in PostgreSQL are invoked with the standalone `CALL procedure_name(...);` statement, not `SELECT`.'},{q:'In a `BEFORE INSERT OR UPDATE` row-level trigger function that calculates `NEW.total`, what must the function return so the modified row is saved?',o:['RETURN OLD;','RETURN NULL;','RETURN NEW;','RETURN TRUE;'],a:2,e:'A `BEFORE` row-level trigger on `INSERT` or `UPDATE` must return `NEW` to proceed with writing the (potentially modified) new row. Returning `NULL` cancels the write for that row.',k:'mcq',w:'A `BEFORE` row-level trigger on `INSERT` or `UPDATE` must return `NEW` to proceed with writing the (potentially modified) new row. Returning `NULL` cancels the write for that row.'},{q:'Why does the `WHEN (OLD.items IS DISTINCT FROM NEW.items)` clause improve the performance of an `UPDATE` trigger on `orders`?',o:['It converts the row-level trigger into a statement-level trigger.','It prevents the trigger function from firing when an UPDATE only changes other columns (like `status`), avoiding redundant recalculation of `total` and Correctly handling NULL comparisons.','It allows the trigger to execute `COMMIT` inside its body.','It bypasses all `CHECK` constraints on the `orders` table.'],a:1,e:'`IS DISTINCT FROM` treats `NULL` safely and ensures `trg_fn_order_total()` only runs when the `items` JSONB column actually changed.',k:'mcq',w:'`IS DISTINCT FROM` treats `NULL` safely and ensures `trg_fn_order_total()` only runs when the `items` JSONB column actually changed.'},{q:'During `CALL make_order(3, items)`, the first item succeeds in deducting stock from `variations`, but the second item tries to deduct 50 units when only 5 are available (violating `CHECK (qty_on_hand >= 0)`). What is the final state of the database?',o:['Item 1\'s stock remains deducted, and a partial order with only Item 1 is inserted.','Item 1\'s stock remains deducted, and no order row is inserted.','The entire procedure call is rolled back atomically: Item 1\'s stock is restored and no order row is inserted.','PostgreSQL sets `qty_on_hand` to 0 for Item 2 and inserts the order.'],a:2,e:'Unless explicit `COMMIT` statements are executed inside the procedure, the entire `CALL` runs atomically; an unhandled constraint violation rolls back all changes made earlier in the loop.',k:'mcq',w:'Unless explicit `COMMIT` statements are executed inside the procedure, the entire `CALL` runs atomically; an unhandled constraint violation rolls back all changes made earlier in the loop.'},{q:'Which of the following is TRUE about a function declared with `RETURNS TABLE(id TEXT, name TEXT)` in PL/pgSQL?',o:['It must be called using `CALL fn();`','Inside the body, you use `RETURN QUERY SELECT ...;` to append rows to the function\'s result set, and callers query it with `SELECT * FROM fn();`','It can only return a single row.','It cannot accept any input parameters.'],a:1,e:'Set-returning functions declared with `RETURNS TABLE(...)` use `RETURN QUERY` in PL/pgSQL and are queried in the `FROM` clause like a regular table.',k:'mcq',w:'Set-returning functions declared with `RETURNS TABLE(...)` use `RETURN QUERY` in PL/pgSQL and are queried in the `FROM` clause like a regular table.'}],
theory:[{q:'Write a trigger that prevents stock from going below zero on products.',pts:['Trigger function','IF NEW.stock < 0','RAISE EXCEPTION','RETURN NEW','BEFORE UPDATE trigger','FOR EACH ROW'],model:`<pre>CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION check_stock() RETURNS trigger AS $$
BEGIN
  IF NEW.stock &lt; 0 THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'Stock cannot be negative'; END IF;
  RETURN NEW;
END; $$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;
CREATE TRIGGER trg_stock BEFORE UPDATE ON products
FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION check_stock();</pre>`},{q:'Exam Q3(a) & (b): (a) Explain the key differences between a SQL function and a stored procedure in PostgreSQL (5 marks). (b) Write: (i) a PL/pgSQL function check_voting_eligibility(p_student_id VARCHAR) that returns \'Eligible\' if a student in the student(id, name, dept_name, dob) table is at least 18 years old and \'Not Eligible\' otherwise (6 marks); and (ii) a SQL function physics_voting_status() that returns the name, dob, age, and voting eligibility of all students in the \'Physics\' department by calling your function from b(i) (3 marks).',model:`<p><b>Background:</b> PostgreSQL provides both user-defined functions (<code>CREATE FUNCTION</code>) and stored procedures (<code>CREATE PROCEDURE</code>) to encapsulate server-side database logic. Choosing between them depends on whether the routine must return a queryable result or orchestrate a multi-statement transactional workflow.</p><p><b>Answer and solution:</b></p><p><b>(a) Differences between a Function and a Stored Procedure (5 marks):</b></p><ol><li><b>Invocation:</b> A function is evaluated inside a SQL query expression (e.g., <code>SELECT fn(x) FROM tbl;</code>), whereas a procedure is invoked as a standalone command using <code>CALL proc(x);</code> and cannot appear inside a <code>SELECT</code> list or <code>WHERE</code> clause.</li><li><b>Return Value:</b> A function must specify a <code>RETURNS</code> type (scalar, composite, <code>TABLE</code>, or <code>VOID</code>) and return a value, whereas a procedure has no return type (though it may use <code>INOUT</code> parameters).</li><li><b>Transaction Control:</b> A function always executes inside the outer query's transaction and cannot run <code>COMMIT</code> or <code>ROLLBACK</code>. A stored procedure can explicitly commit or roll back transactions within its body.</li><li><b>Side Effects vs Computation:</b> Functions are designed for reusable computations and table-producing lookups, whereas procedures are designed for multi-step write operations across tables.</li></ol><p><b>(b)(i) PL/pgSQL Function <code>check_voting_eligibility</code> (6 marks):</b></p><pre>CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION check_voting_eligibility(p_student_id VARCHAR)
RETURNS TEXT AS $$
DECLARE
  v_age INT;
BEGIN
  SELECT EXTRACT(YEAR FROM AGE(CURRENT_DATE, dob))::INT
  INTO v_age
  FROM student
  WHERE id = p_student_id;

  IF NOT FOUND THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'Student with ID % not found', p_student_id;
  END IF;

  IF v_age &gt;= 18 THEN
    RETURN 'Eligible';
  ELSE
    RETURN 'Not Eligible';
  END IF;
END;
$$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;</pre><p><b>(b)(ii) SQL Table Function for Physics Students (3 marks):</b></p><pre>CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION physics_voting_status()
RETURNS TABLE(
  student_name VARCHAR,
  date_of_birth DATE,
  student_age INT,
  eligibility TEXT
) AS $$
  SELECT
    s.name,
    s.dob,
    EXTRACT(YEAR FROM AGE(CURRENT_DATE, s.dob))::INT AS student_age,
    check_voting_eligibility(s.id) AS eligibility
  FROM student s
  WHERE s.dept_name = 'Physics';
$$ LANGUAGE sql;</pre><p><b>Scenario:</b> Suppose student <code>'12345'</code> (Adaobi, in Physics) has <code>dob = '2006-03-10'</code> and student <code>'67890'</code> (Tunde, in Physics) has <code>dob = '2010-09-15'</code>. Running <code>SELECT * FROM physics_voting_status();</code> computes their ages using PostgreSQL's <code>AGE(CURRENT_DATE, s.dob)</code> interval function and calls <code>check_voting_eligibility(s.id)</code> for each row.</p><p><b>How they combine:</b> The PL/pgSQL function in b(i) uses procedural variables (<code>v_age</code>), <code>SELECT ... INTO</code>, <code>IF NOT FOUND</code> validation, and branching logic to classify a single student. The declarative <code>LANGUAGE sql</code> function in b(ii) composes that scalar function directly inside a set-returning query over the <code>Physics</code> department.</p><p><b>Conclusion:</b> Separating row-level business rules (<code>check_voting_eligibility</code>) from department-level reporting queries (<code>physics_voting_status</code>) keeps SQL logic modular, reusable, and easy to maintain.</p>`,rubric:['Part (a): Contrasts invocation (SELECT vs CALL), return values (RETURNS clause vs no return value), transaction control (COMMIT/ROLLBACK allowed in procedures only), and usage context','Part (b-i): Uses LANGUAGE plpgsql with DECLARE, SELECT EXTRACT(YEAR FROM AGE(CURRENT_DATE, dob)) INTO, checks IF NOT FOUND, and returns Eligible when age >= 18 else Not Eligible','Part (b-ii): Defines a function returning name, dob, age, and check_voting_eligibility(s.id) filtered by WHERE dept_name = \'Physics\''],pts:['Part (a): Contrasts invocation (SELECT vs CALL), return values (RETURNS clause vs no return value), transaction control (COMMIT/ROLLBACK allowed in procedures only), and usage context','Part (b-i): Uses LANGUAGE plpgsql with DECLARE, SELECT EXTRACT(YEAR FROM AGE(CURRENT_DATE, dob)) INTO, checks IF NOT FOUND, and returns Eligible when age >= 18 else Not Eligible','Part (b-ii): Defines a function returning name, dob, age, and check_voting_eligibility(s.id) filtered by WHERE dept_name = \'Physics\'']},{q:'Exam Q5(b): Given the E-Commerce schema with products(product_id, product_info, price, manufacturer_id) and orders(orderid, customer_id, orderdetails jsonb, totalamount, orderdate), where orderdetails is a JSONB array like \'[{"product_id": 7, "quantity": 1}, {"product_id": 9, "quantity": 4}]\', write: (i) a helper function compute_total(p_items JSONB) that looks up each product\'s price from the products table and returns the total order amount (7.5 marks); and (ii) a stored procedure make_orders(p_user_id INT, p_items JSONB) that generates orderid by concatenating the current timestamp with p_user_id, calls compute_total, and inserts the new order (5 marks).',model:`<p><b>Background:</b> In the Exam Q5(b) schema (unlike the lecture variation where <code>unit_price</code> is already inside the JSONB or stored on a <code>variations</code> table), each element of the input JSONB array contains only <code>product_id</code> and <code>quantity</code>. The helper function must therefore unpack the JSONB array with <code>jsonb_array_elements</code> and join against <code>products.price</code>.</p><p><b>Answer and solution:</b></p><p><b>(b)(i) Helper Function <code>compute_total(p_items JSONB)</code> (7.5 marks):</b></p><pre>CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION compute_total(p_items JSONB)
RETURNS NUMERIC(12, 2) AS $$
DECLARE
  v_total NUMERIC(12, 2);
BEGIN
  SELECT COALESCE(
    SUM(p.price * (elem-&gt;&gt;'quantity')::INT),
    0
  )
  INTO v_total
  FROM jsonb_array_elements(p_items) AS elem
  JOIN products p
    ON p.product_id = (elem-&gt;&gt;'product_id')::INT;

  RETURN v_total;
END;
$$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;</pre><p><b>(b)(ii) Stored Procedure <code>make_orders(p_user_id INT, p_items JSONB)</code> (5 marks):</b></p><pre>CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE make_orders(p_user_id INT, p_items JSONB)
LANGUAGE plpgsql AS $$
DECLARE
  v_order_id TEXT;
  v_total NUMERIC(12, 2);
BEGIN
  -- Concatenate current timestamp with user ID
  v_order_id := TO_CHAR(NOW(), 'YYYYMMDDHH24MISS') || '-' || p_user_id::TEXT;

  -- Compute total using the helper function from b(i)
  v_total := compute_total(p_items);

  -- Insert the new order record
  INSERT INTO orders (orderid, customer_id, orderdetails, totalamount, orderdate)
  VALUES (v_order_id, p_user_id, p_items, v_total, NOW());
END;
$$;</pre><p><b>Scenario:</b> When the user executes Figure 4's call:</p><pre>CALL make_orders(
  3,
  '[{"product_id": 7, "quantity": 1}, {"product_id": 9, "quantity": 4}, {"product_id": 11, "quantity": 2}]'::jsonb
);</pre><p><code>compute_total</code> expands the 3 JSON objects, extracts <code>(elem-&gt;&gt;'product_id')::INT</code> and <code>(elem-&gt;&gt;'quantity')::INT</code>, joins each with <code>products.price</code>, and returns the exact sum.</p><p><b>How they combine:</b> <code>compute_total</code> encapsulates the JSONB-to-relational join logic in a reusable function, while <code>make_orders</code> handles the procedural workflow of generating the composite timestamp-user ID, invoking <code>compute_total</code>, and inserting the committed row into <code>orders</code>.</p><p><b>Conclusion:</b> Using <code>jsonb_array_elements</code> with text extraction (<code>-&gt;&gt;</code>) and explicit integer casts bridges semi-structured JSONB payloads with strongly typed relational price lookups in a single atomic procedure call.</p>`,rubric:['Part (b-i): Expands p_items with jsonb_array_elements(p_items), extracts product_id and quantity using ->> with ::INT cast, joins products on product_id, and sums p.price * quantity','Part (b-ii): Uses CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE make_orders(p_user_id INT, p_items JSONB), concatenates current timestamp and p_user_id for orderid, calls compute_total(p_items), and inserts into orders'],pts:['Part (b-i): Expands p_items with jsonb_array_elements(p_items), extracts product_id and quantity using ->> with ::INT cast, joins products on product_id, and sums p.price * quantity','Part (b-ii): Uses CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE make_orders(p_user_id INT, p_items JSONB), concatenates current timestamp and p_user_id for orderid, calls compute_total(p_items), and inserts into orders']}]},
{id:'t5',title:'SQL practice workbook',why:'Joins, aggregation, subqueries and CTEs: the bread and butter of the exam.',
cards:[
{h:'Core patterns',b:`<pre>SELECT c.name, COUNT(o.id) AS orders, SUM(o.total) AS spent
FROM customers c LEFT JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.id
GROUP BY c.name HAVING SUM(o.total) &gt; 1000
ORDER BY spent DESC LIMIT 5;

WITH monthly AS (
  SELECT date_trunc('month', created_at) m, SUM(total) s FROM orders GROUP BY 1)
SELECT * FROM monthly WHERE s &gt; (SELECT AVG(s) FROM monthly);</pre><p>WHERE filters rows before grouping; HAVING filters groups. LEFT JOIN keeps unmatched left rows. Window: <code>RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY dept ORDER BY salary DESC)</code>.</p>`},
{h:'CA1 Practical Test Walkthrough (Q1 to Q5): Core SQL & Indexing',b:`<p>The 30-mark CSC420 Practical Test uses the <b><code>business_db</code></b> schema (<code>customers</code>, <code>products</code>, <code>orders</code>):</p><ul><li><b>Q1 (2m — Simple Filtering):</b> <code>SELECT customer_name, city, state_code, acct_balance FROM customers WHERE state_code = 'KAN' AND segment = 'Corporate' ORDER BY acct_balance DESC;</code></li><li><b>Q2 (3m — Grouping &amp; Aggregation):</b> <code>SELECT p.category, SUM(o.quantity_ordered) AS total_qty, SUM(o.order_total) AS total_revenue FROM orders o JOIN products p USING (product_id) WHERE o.status = 'Completed' GROUP BY p.category ORDER BY total_revenue DESC;</code></li><li><b>Q3 (4m — LEFT JOIN Zero-Order Filter):</b> <code>SELECT c.customer_id, c.customer_name, c.segment, c.state_code FROM customers c LEFT JOIN orders o USING (customer_id) WHERE o.order_id IS NULL ORDER BY c.customer_id;</code></li><li><b>Q4 (5m — CTE Revenue Above Category Average):</b> Compute per-product revenue in a CTE <code>product_rev</code>, then filter where <code>prod_rev &gt; AVG(prod_rev)</code> within the same category using either a second CTE <code>cat_avg</code> or a window function <code>AVG(prod_rev) OVER (PARTITION BY category)</code>.</li><li><b>Q5 (4m — Composite B+-Tree Index):</b> <code>CREATE INDEX idx_orders_status_date ON orders (status, order_date);</code> accelerates queries with equality on <code>status</code> and a range on <code>order_date</code>.</li></ul>`},
{h:'CA1 Practical Test Walkthrough (Q6): Array Replace & unnest() Aggregation',b:`<p>Question 6 (6 marks) tests two essential PostgreSQL array patterns on <code>customers.interests TEXT[]</code>:</p><p><b>Q6-1 (3m):</b> For customers whose <code>interests</code> contain <code>'Groceries'</code>, remove <code>'Groceries'</code> and add <code>'Laptops'</code> &amp; <code>'Phones &amp; Tablets'</code>:</p><pre>UPDATE customers
SET interests = array_cat(
  array_remove(interests, 'Groceries'),
  ARRAY['Laptops', 'Phones &amp; Tablets']
)
WHERE 'Groceries' = ANY(interests);</pre><p><b>Q6-2 (3m):</b> Top 5 most common interests among <code>'Wholesale'</code> customers (breaking ties alphabetically):</p><pre>SELECT interest, COUNT(*) AS customer_count
FROM customers,
LATERAL unnest(interests) AS interest
WHERE segment = 'Wholesale'
GROUP BY interest
ORDER BY customer_count DESC, interest ASC
LIMIT 5;</pre>`},
{h:'CA1 Practical Test Walkthrough (Q7): Correlated Array Sum & FILTER',b:`<p>Question 7 (6 marks) asks for a single-row summary of <b>2025 orders for <code>'Electronics'</code> products by customers in <code>'LAG'</code></b>, where each order has an integer array <code>deliveries INT[]</code> recording batch delivery quantities:</p><ul><li>An order is <b>incompletely delivered</b> if <code>cardinality(o.deliveries) = 0</code> OR the sum of elements in <code>o.deliveries</code> is less than <code>o.quantity_ordered</code>.</li></ul><pre>SELECT
  COUNT(*) AS total_orders,
  COALESCE(SUM(o.quantity_ordered), 0) AS total_quantity_ordered,
  COALESCE(SUM((
    SELECT COALESCE(SUM(x), 0) FROM unnest(o.deliveries) AS x
  )), 0) AS total_quantity_delivered,
  COUNT(*) FILTER (
    WHERE COALESCE((
      SELECT SUM(x) FROM unnest(o.deliveries) AS x
    ), 0) &lt; o.quantity_ordered
  ) AS incomplete_orders
FROM orders o
JOIN customers c USING (customer_id)
JOIN products p USING (product_id)
WHERE p.category = 'Electronics'
  AND c.state_code = 'LAG'
  AND o.order_date &gt;= DATE '2025-01-01'
  AND o.order_date &lt;  DATE '2026-01-01';</pre>`},
{h:'Exam Q3(c): Table Function + Mentees of Lowest Earner per Department',b:`<p>In Exam Q3(c), Figure 1 defines <code>instructor_of(dname text) RETURNS TABLE(id varchar, name varchar, dept_name varchar, salary numeric)</code> returning all instructors in department <code>dname</code>, alongside tables <code>instructor</code>, <code>student</code>, and <code>mentoring(studentid, instructorid)</code>.</p><p><b>(c)(i) Fetch the IDs of the mentees of the lowest earner in each department (4 marks):</b></p><pre>SELECT i.dept_name, i.id AS instructor_id, i.name AS instructor_name, m.studentid AS mentee_id
FROM instructor i
JOIN mentoring m ON m.instructorid = i.id
WHERE i.salary = (
  SELECT MIN(sub.salary)
  FROM instructor_of(i.dept_name) AS sub
);</pre><p><b>(c)(ii) Tweak needed if the names of the mentees are also required (2 marks):</b></p><p>Add a <code>JOIN student s ON s.id = m.studentid</code> and include <code>s.name AS mentee_name</code> in the <code>SELECT</code> list.</p>`}],
recall:[['WHERE vs HAVING?','WHERE filters rows before grouping; HAVING filters groups after aggregation.'],['INNER vs LEFT JOIN?','INNER keeps only matches; LEFT keeps all left rows with NULLs where unmatched.'],['What is a CTE?','A named temporary result defined with WITH and used in the main query.'],['In PostgreSQL, how do you replace one element (\'Groceries\') with two new elements (\'Laptops\', \'Phones & Tablets\') inside a TEXT[] column?','Use UPDATE customers SET interests = array_cat(array_remove(interests, \'Groceries\'), ARRAY[\'Laptops\', \'Phones & Tablets\']) WHERE \'Groceries\' = ANY(interests);'],['How do you sum the integer elements inside an array column o.deliveries INT[] for each row in an outer query?','Use a correlated scalar subquery with unnest: COALESCE((SELECT SUM(x) FROM unnest(o.deliveries) AS x), 0).'],['In CA1 Question 3, why is LEFT JOIN ... WHERE o.order_id IS NULL used instead of an INNER JOIN?','An INNER JOIN drops customers with zero orders. A LEFT JOIN preserves all customers and fills order columns with NULL when no matching order exists, so WHERE o.order_id IS NULL isolates customers who have never placed an order.'],['In Exam Q3(c), if you already have a query joining instructor i and mentoring m on m.instructorid = i.id to get mentee IDs (m.studentid), what tweak retrieves the mentees\' names as well?','Join the student table on the mentee ID (JOIN student s ON s.id = m.studentid) and add s.name to the SELECT list.']],
quiz:[{k:'mcq',q:'Customers with no orders appear with…',o:['INNER JOIN','LEFT JOIN from customers','CROSS JOIN','RIGHT JOIN from customers'],a:1},{k:'tf',q:'Aggregate conditions like SUM(total) > 100 go in WHERE.',a:false},{q:'Which query correctly finds the top 5 most common individual interests stored in `customers.interests TEXT[]` among `\'Wholesale\'` customers?',o:['SELECT interests, COUNT(*) FROM customers WHERE segment = \'Wholesale\' GROUP BY interests LIMIT 5;','SELECT interest, COUNT(*) AS cnt FROM customers, LATERAL unnest(interests) AS interest WHERE segment = \'Wholesale\' GROUP BY interest ORDER BY cnt DESC, interest ASC LIMIT 5;','SELECT cardinality(interests), COUNT(*) FROM customers GROUP BY 1 LIMIT 5;','SELECT jsonb_array_elements(interests) FROM customers WHERE segment = \'Wholesale\';'],a:1,e:'To count individual elements inside a `TEXT[]` column across rows, you must expand the array into one row per element using `unnest(interests) AS interest` and then `GROUP BY interest`.',k:'mcq',w:'To count individual elements inside a `TEXT[]` column across rows, you must expand the array into one row per element using `unnest(interests) AS interest` and then `GROUP BY interest`.'},{q:'In CA1 Question 5, why is `CREATE INDEX idx_orders_status_date ON orders (status, order_date);` ordered with `status` first and `order_date` second for `WHERE status = \'Completed\' AND order_date >= \'2025-01-01\'`?',o:['Alphabetical column ordering is required by PostgreSQL.','In a composite B+-tree index, placing the equality column (`status`) first narrows the tree to the exact status slice, within which the second column (`order_date`) is sorted contiguously for the range scan.','B+-tree indexes only index the last column listed.','PostgreSQL cannot place a DATE column first in an index.'],a:1,e:'Composite B+-tree indexes follow the equality-before-range rule: matching `status = \'Completed\'` on the leading key isolates a contiguous range of `order_date` entries.',k:'mcq',w:'Composite B+-tree indexes follow the equality-before-range rule: matching `status = \'Completed\'` on the leading key isolates a contiguous range of `order_date` entries.'}],
theory:[{q:'Write a query for the top 3 products by revenue in 2026 with their category name.',pts:['JOIN order_items/products/categories','Date filter','SUM(qty*price)','GROUP BY','ORDER BY DESC','LIMIT 3'],model:`<pre>SELECT p.name, cat.name AS category, SUM(oi.qty*oi.price) AS revenue
FROM order_items oi
JOIN orders o ON o.id = oi.order_id
JOIN products p ON p.id = oi.product_id
JOIN categories cat ON cat.id = p.category_id
WHERE o.created_at &gt;= '2026-01-01' AND o.created_at &lt; '2027-01-01'
GROUP BY p.name, cat.name
ORDER BY revenue DESC LIMIT 3;</pre>`},{q:'CA1 Practical Test Q6 & Q7: On the business_db database (customers, products, orders), write PostgreSQL statements to: (a) remove \'Groceries\' and append \'Laptops\' and \'Phones & Tablets\' for every customer interested in \'Groceries\', and list the top 5 interests among \'Wholesale\' customers; and (b) produce a single-row 2025 delivery summary (total_orders, total_quantity_ordered, total_quantity_delivered, incomplete_orders) for \'Electronics\' products ordered by customers in \'LAG\'.',model:`<p><b>Background:</b> PostgreSQL native arrays (<code>TEXT[]</code> and <code>INT[]</code>) allow multi-valued attributes such as customer interests and batch delivery quantities to be stored directly inside relational rows without creating separate junction tables. Querying and mutating these arrays requires array manipulation functions (<code>array_remove</code>, <code>array_cat</code>, <code>ANY</code>) and set-returning expansion (<code>unnest</code>).</p><p><b>Answer and solution:</b></p><p><b>(a) Part 1 — Updating the <code>interests</code> array (3 marks):</b></p><pre>UPDATE customers
SET interests = array_cat(
  array_remove(interests, 'Groceries'),
  ARRAY['Laptops', 'Phones &amp; Tablets']
)
WHERE 'Groceries' = ANY(interests);</pre><p><b>(a) Part 2 — Top 5 interests for Wholesale customers (3 marks):</b></p><pre>SELECT
  interest,
  COUNT(*) AS customer_count
FROM customers,
LATERAL unnest(interests) AS interest
WHERE segment = 'Wholesale'
GROUP BY interest
ORDER BY customer_count DESC, interest ASC
LIMIT 5;</pre><p><b>(b) 2025 Electronics Delivery Summary for Lagos (<code>'LAG'</code>) (6 marks):</b></p><pre>SELECT
  COUNT(*) AS total_orders,
  COALESCE(SUM(o.quantity_ordered), 0) AS total_quantity_ordered,
  COALESCE(SUM((
    SELECT COALESCE(SUM(x), 0)
    FROM unnest(o.deliveries) AS x
  )), 0) AS total_quantity_delivered,
  COUNT(*) FILTER (
    WHERE COALESCE((
      SELECT SUM(x)
      FROM unnest(o.deliveries) AS x
    ), 0) &lt; o.quantity_ordered
  ) AS incomplete_orders
FROM orders o
JOIN customers c USING (customer_id)
JOIN products p USING (product_id)
WHERE p.category = 'Electronics'
  AND c.state_code = 'LAG'
  AND o.order_date &gt;= DATE '2025-01-01'
  AND o.order_date &lt;  DATE '2026-01-01';</pre><p><b>Scenario:</b> Suppose an order in Lagos for 10 laptops has <code>deliveries = '{4, 3}'</code> (sum = 7 &lt; 10) and another for 5 phones has <code>deliveries = '{}'</code> (empty array, sum = 0 &lt; 5). The correlated subquery <code>(SELECT COALESCE(SUM(x), 0) FROM unnest(o.deliveries) AS x)</code> evaluates to <code>7</code> and <code>0</code> respectively, so both are counted by the <code>FILTER (WHERE ... &lt; o.quantity_ordered)</code> clause as incomplete orders.</p><p><b>How they combine:</b> Avoid expanding <code>unnest(o.deliveries)</code> directly in the outer <code>FROM</code> clause of part (b): doing so would drop orders with empty <code>'{}'</code> delivery arrays and multiply <code>o.quantity_ordered</code> by the number of delivery batches. Evaluating <code>unnest(o.deliveries)</code> inside a correlated scalar subquery preserves a strict one-row-per-order grain before outer aggregation.</p><p><b>Conclusion:</b> Combining <code>unnest()</code> inside correlated subqueries with <code>COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE ...)</code> produces accurate multi-metric summaries over array columns in a single SQL pass.</p>`,rubric:['Uses array_remove and array_cat (or ||) filtered by \'Groceries\' = ANY(interests) or @> ARRAY[\'Groceries\']','Expands interests with unnest(interests) filtered by segment = \'Wholesale\', groups by interest, orders by count DESC, interest ASC, and limits to 5','Computes per-order delivery sums safely for empty arrays using COALESCE and a correlated unnest(o.deliveries) subquery without duplicating outer order rows'],pts:['Uses array_remove and array_cat (or ||) filtered by \'Groceries\' = ANY(interests) or @> ARRAY[\'Groceries\']','Expands interests with unnest(interests) filtered by segment = \'Wholesale\', groups by interest, orders by count DESC, interest ASC, and limits to 5','Computes per-order delivery sums safely for empty arrays using COALESCE and a correlated unnest(o.deliveries) subquery without duplicating outer order rows']}]},
{id:'t6',title:'NoSQL data models',why:'Four NoSQL models, CAP and BASE.',
cards:[
{h:'Models',b:`<table><tr><th>Model</th><th>Example</th><th>Use</th></tr><tr><td>Key–value</td><td>Redis</td><td>Caching, sessions</td></tr><tr><td>Document</td><td>MongoDB</td><td>Flexible JSON records</td></tr><tr><td>Column-family</td><td>Cassandra</td><td>Huge write-heavy data</td></tr><tr><td>Graph</td><td>Neo4j</td><td>Relationships, recommendations</td></tr></table><p><b>CAP theorem</b>: a distributed system can guarantee at most two of Consistency, Availability, Partition tolerance. <b>BASE</b>: Basically Available, Soft state, Eventual consistency (vs ACID).</p>`},
{h:'Origins of NoSQL & The 3 Vs of Big Data',b:`<p>The term <b>NoSQL</b> gained popularity from a <b>2009 San Francisco meetup</b> organized using a short Twitter hashtag (<code>#nosql</code>) for open-source, distributed, non-relational databases. Today it means <b>"Not Only SQL"</b>.</p><ul><li><b>3 Vs driving NoSQL:</b><ul><li><b>Volume:</b> Terabytes/petabytes of user and sensor data exceeding a single server.</li><li><b>Velocity:</b> Millions of concurrent reads and writes per second requiring low latency.</li><li><b>Variety:</b> Semi-structured and polymorphic data (nested JSON, social graphs, logs) that resist rigid relational schemas.</li></ul></li><li><b>Pioneering systems:</b> Google <b>Bigtable</b> (2006), Amazon <b>Dynamo</b> (2007), and Facebook <b>Cassandra</b> (2008).</li></ul>`},
{h:'Model 1: Key-Value Stores & Redis Cart Commands',b:`<p>A <b>Key-Value store</b> (e.g., <b>Redis</b>, Amazon DynamoDB, Memcached) is a distributed hash table mapping a unique key to an opaque value (string, hash, list, set). Lookups by key are $O(1)$.</p><p><b>Lecture 4 Redis Shopping Cart Example (User 3, Product 7 Variation 2):</b></p><pre>HSET cart:3 7:2 2        -- Set field "7:2" to quantity 2 in hash cart:3
HINCRBY cart:3 7:2 1     -- Increment quantity of "7:2" by 1 (now 3)
HGETALL cart:3           -- Return all item fields and quantities in cart:3
EXPIRE cart:3 86400      -- Auto-delete cart:3 after 24 hours (86,400 seconds)</pre><div class="tip"><b>Limitation:</b> You cannot query inside values across keys (e.g., "find all carts containing Indomie") without scanning every key or maintaining a secondary index.</div>`},
{h:'Model 2: Document Databases (MongoDB) & Aggregate Independence',b:`<p>A <b>Document database</b> (e.g., <b>MongoDB</b>, CouchDB, Firestore) stores self-contained JSON/BSON documents grouped into collections.</p><pre>db.products.find(
  { "variations.price": { $lt: 3000 } },
  { name: 1, _id: 0 }
)</pre><ul><li>Unlike Key-Value stores, document databases understand the internal structure of each document and allow secondary indexes and queries on any nested field.</li><li><b>Why documents scale out easily:</b> Because an entire product (with its variations) or an entire order is stored together as one self-contained unit, documents can be partitioned (sharded) across servers with minimal cross-node joins.</li></ul>`},
{h:'Model 3: Column-Family / Wide-Column Stores (Apache Cassandra)',b:`<p>Modelled on Google Bigtable, <b>Wide-Column stores</b> (e.g., <b>Apache Cassandra</b>, HBase) organize data into tables where the <b>PRIMARY KEY</b> has two parts:</p><pre>CREATE TABLE orders_by_user (
  user_id int,
  order_id text,
  created_at timestamp,
  total int,
  status text,
  PRIMARY KEY ((user_id), order_id)
);</pre><ul><li><b>Partition Key <code>(user_id)</code>:</b> Hashed to determine which node in the cluster stores all orders for that user.</li><li><b>Clustering Column <code>order_id</code>:</b> Sorts all orders for that user physically on disk inside the partition.</li><li><b>Query-driven design:</b> Looking up <code>WHERE user_id = 3</code> reads one contiguous partition on one node. Querying without the partition key (e.g., <code>WHERE status = 'Pending'</code>) is <b>rejected by Cassandra</b> unless you append <code>ALLOW FILTERING</code>, which forces a slow full-cluster scan!</li></ul>`},
{h:'Model 4: Graph Databases (Neo4j Cypher & 2024 ISO GQL)',b:`<p><b>Graph databases</b> (e.g., <b>Neo4j</b>) store <b>nodes</b> (entities with labels like <code>:User</code>, <code>:Product</code>) and directed <b>relationships</b> (<code>-[:BOUGHT]-&gt;</code>) with index-free adjacency. In <b>April 2024</b>, <b>ISO GQL</b> became the first new ISO database query language standard since SQL (1987).</p><p><b>Lecture 4 Collaborative Filtering Recommendation in Cypher:</b></p><pre>MATCH (a:User {name: "Adaeze"})-[:BOUGHT]-&gt;(:Product)
      &lt;-[:BOUGHT]-(:User)-[:BOUGHT]-&gt;(rec:Product)
WHERE NOT EXISTS { MATCH (a)-[:BOUGHT]-&gt;(rec) }
RETURN DISTINCT rec.name;</pre><p><b>University DB 3-hop Traversal (Appendix):</b></p><pre>MATCH (s:Student)-[:TAKES]-&gt;(sc:Section)
      &lt;-[:TEACHES]-(i:Instructor)-[:BELONGS_TO]-&gt;(d:Department)
WHERE d.deptname = 'Comp. Sci.'
RETURN s, sc;</pre><div class="tip"><b>Why graphs are hardest to shard:</b> Partitioning a connected graph across servers cuts edges between nodes (minimum edge-cut is NP-hard), causing multi-hop traversals to suffer high cross-network latency ("network hop per relationship").</div>`},
{h:'Schema-on-Write vs Schema-on-Read, ACID vs BASE & CAP',b:`<table><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Relational (SQL)</th><th>NoSQL (Distributed)</th></tr><tr><td><b>Schema Enforcement</b></td><td><b>Schema-on-Write:</b> Database validates columns and types before allowing an <code>INSERT</code>.</td><td><b>Schema-on-Read:</b> Database accepts any document fields on write; application interprets structure when reading.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Consistency Model</b></td><td><b>ACID:</b> Immediate strong consistency across multi-table transactions.</td><td><b>BASE:</b> <b>B</b>asically <b>A</b>vailable, <b>S</b>oft state, <b>E</b>ventually consistent across replicas.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Scaling</b></td><td><b>Scale Up (Vertical):</b> Bigger CPU/RAM/SSD on one server.</td><td><b>Scale Out (Horizontal):</b> <b>Sharding</b> (splitting rows across nodes by key range/hash) + <b>Replication</b> (copies for fault tolerance).</td></tr></table><p><b>CAP Theorem (Brewer):</b> When a <b>Network Partition (P)</b> occurs between nodes, a distributed database must choose between <b>Consistency (C)</b> (reject writes/reads that cannot reach quorum) and <b>Availability (A)</b> (accept writes/reads on isolated nodes, risking stale data until the partition heals).</p>`},
{h:'Polyglot Persistence, Blurring Lines, NewSQL & Vector DBs',b:`<ul><li><b>Polyglot Persistence:</b> Using different database models inside the same system for what each does best — e.g., Splendid Stores using <b>PostgreSQL</b> for payments/inventory, <b>Redis</b> for ephemeral shopping carts, <b>MongoDB</b> for flexible product catalogs, <b>Cassandra</b> for order history logs, and <b>Neo4j</b> for product recommendations.</li><li><b>Blurring Lines:</b> Relational DBs added JSONB/arrays (PostgreSQL); NoSQL added SQL-like query languages (Cassandra CQL) and multi-document ACID transactions (MongoDB 4.0+); <b>NewSQL</b> (Google Spanner, CockroachDB) provides horizontal scale-out with full SQL and ACID; Michael Stonebraker (2010) argued NoSQL speed gains come primarily from removing classic locking/logging/buffer-pool overhead.</li><li><b>Other Specialized Models (Slide 35 &amp; Course Outline):</b><ul><li><b>Time-Series:</b> InfluxDB, Prometheus (IoT/metrics indexed by timestamp).</li><li><b>Search Engines:</b> Elasticsearch, OpenSearch (inverted indexes for full-text search).</li><li><b>Vector Databases:</b> <b>Pinecone, Milvus, Qdrant, pgvector</b> — store high-dimensional dense numerical vectors (embeddings from LLMs/neural networks) and perform fast $k$-Nearest Neighbour ($k$-NN) / Approximate Nearest Neighbour (ANN) similarity searches using <b>cosine similarity</b>, <b>dot product</b>, or <b>Euclidean ($L_2$) distance</b> for semantic search and RAG.</li><li><b>Multi-model:</b> ArangoDB, SurrealDB.</li></ul></li></ul>`}],
recall:[['Four NoSQL models.','Key–value, document, column-family, graph.'],['State CAP.','Of consistency, availability and partition tolerance, a distributed system can guarantee only two at once.'],['Expand BASE.','Basically Available, Soft state, Eventual consistency.'],['Where did the term "NoSQL" originate in 2009, and what does it stand for today?','It originated as a short Twitter hashtag (#nosql) chosen by Johan Oskarsson for a 2009 San Francisco meetup on open-source, distributed, non-relational databases. Today it stands for "Not Only SQL".'],['Write the four Redis commands from Lecture 4 to add 2 units of variation "7:2" to user 3\'s cart, increment it by 1, fetch the whole cart, and set a 24-hour expiry.','HSET cart:3 7:2 2; HINCRBY cart:3 7:2 1; HGETALL cart:3; EXPIRE cart:3 86400'],['In Apache Cassandra\'s PRIMARY KEY ((user_id), order_id), what are the roles of user_id and order_id, and why does WHERE status = \'Pending\' fail by default?','user_id is the partition key (hashed to pick which cluster node stores the partition) and order_id is the clustering column (sorts rows inside that partition). Filtering by status without the partition key requires scanning every node in the cluster, so Cassandra blocks it unless ALLOW FILTERING is specified.'],['Why is horizontal sharding much harder for a Graph database (Neo4j) than for a Document database (MongoDB)?','Documents are self-contained aggregates that can be placed on independent shards by document key. Graphs consist of densely interconnected nodes; splitting a graph across servers cuts edges between machines, forcing multi-hop traversals to make slow network hops at each step.'],['Contrast Schema-on-Write with Schema-on-Read.','Schema-on-Write (relational DBs) enforces table structure, column types, and constraints before data is written to disk. Schema-on-Read (document/key-value NoSQL) stores arbitrary structure immediately on write and defers parsing/validation until the application reads the data.'],['What do the letters in BASE stand for, and how does BASE contrast with ACID?','Basically Available, Soft state, Eventual consistency. Whereas ACID prioritizes immediate strong consistency across every transaction, BASE prioritizes high availability and partition tolerance by allowing replicas to converge asynchronously over time.'],['What is a Vector Database (e.g., Pinecone, Milvus), and how does it search data differently from SQL equality/range queries?','A vector database stores high-dimensional numerical embedding vectors generated by machine-learning models and retrieves semantically similar items using Approximate Nearest Neighbour (ANN) searches over distance metrics such as cosine similarity, Euclidean distance, or inner product.']],
quiz:[{k:'mcq',q:'A social network\'s friend-of-friend queries suit…',o:['Key–value','Graph','Column-family','Relational only'],a:1},{k:'tf',q:'BASE systems favour strict immediate consistency.',a:false},{q:'In Apache Cassandra, given `PRIMARY KEY ((user_id), order_id)`, which statement about querying `SELECT * FROM orders_by_user WHERE status = \'Delivered\';` is TRUE?',o:['Cassandra automatically uses a B+-tree index on `status`.','Cassandra rejects the query by default because it omits the partition key `user_id`; running it requires `ALLOW FILTERING`, which performs an expensive full-cluster scan.','Cassandra hashes `status` as a secondary partition key automatically.','The query returns `0` rows without an error.'],a:1,e:'In Cassandra, queries must supply the partition key (`user_id`) so the coordinator knows which node holds the data. Omitting it is rejected unless `ALLOW FILTERING` is added.',k:'mcq',w:'In Cassandra, queries must supply the partition key (`user_id`) so the coordinator knows which node holds the data. Omitting it is rejected unless `ALLOW FILTERING` is added.'},{q:'Which Redis command sets an automatic time-to-live of 24 hours on the hash key `cart:3`?',o:['TTL cart:3 24','EXPIRE cart:3 86400','HSET cart:3 expire 86400','TIMEOUT cart:3 86400'],a:1,e:'`EXPIRE key seconds` sets a time-to-live in seconds ($24 \\times 60 \\times 60 = 86400$).',k:'mcq',w:'`EXPIRE key seconds` sets a time-to-live in seconds ($24 \\times 60 \\times 60 = 86400$).'},{q:'In Neo4j Cypher, what does the pattern `(a:User {name: "Adaeze"})-[:BOUGHT]->(:Product)<-[:BOUGHT]-(:User)-[:BOUGHT]->(rec:Product)` match?',o:['Only products that Adaeze has already bought.','Products bought by other users who also bought at least one product that Adaeze bought (collaborative filtering candidates).','Users who have never bought any product.','All products in the database regardless of purchases.'],a:1,e:'The path goes from Adaeze to a product she bought (`-[:BOUGHT]->(:Product)`), backwards to another user who bought that same product (`<-[:BOUGHT]-(:User)`), and forward to another product that user bought (`-[:BOUGHT]->(rec:Product)`).',k:'mcq',w:'The path goes from Adaeze to a product she bought (`-[:BOUGHT]->(:Product)`), backwards to another user who bought that same product (`<-[:BOUGHT]-(:User)`), and forward to another product that user bought (`-[:BOUGHT]->(rec:Product)`).'},{q:'According to the CAP theorem, what trade-off must a distributed database make when a network partition (P) separates cluster nodes?',o:['It must choose between B+-tree indexing and Hash indexing.','It must choose between Consistency (rejecting operations that cannot guarantee up-to-date data) and Availability (serving every request even if some nodes return stale data).','It must choose between Schema-on-Write and Schema-on-Read.','It must stop all reads and writes permanently.'],a:1,e:'Because network partitions (`P`) are inevitable in distributed networks, a system must choose between `C` (Consistency) and `A` (Availability) during a partition.',k:'mcq',w:'Because network partitions (`P`) are inevitable in distributed networks, a system must choose between `C` (Consistency) and `A` (Availability) during a partition.'},{q:'What is "Polyglot Persistence"?',o:['Writing stored procedures in multiple programming languages inside PostgreSQL.','Combining multiple database models (e.g., PostgreSQL + Redis + MongoDB + Neo4j) within one application architecture so each workload uses the data model best suited to it.','Translating SQL queries automatically into Cypher and MQL.','Storing strings in multiple human languages using UTF-8.'],a:1,e:'Polyglot persistence means choosing different data stores for different subsystems (e.g., Redis for carts, Postgres for payments, Neo4j for recommendations) within the same application.',k:'mcq',w:'Polyglot persistence means choosing different data stores for different subsystems (e.g., Redis for carts, Postgres for payments, Neo4j for recommendations) within the same application.'}],
theory:[{q:'Compare relational databases with NoSQL and recommend a model for a session cache and a product catalogue.',pts:['Schema rigid vs flexible','ACID vs BASE','Scaling vertical vs horizontal','Session cache: key–value','Catalogue: document','Justification'],model:`<p>Relational: fixed schema, joins, ACID, vertical scaling. NoSQL: flexible schemas, horizontal scaling, often BASE. Session cache → Redis key–value (fast lookups by id); product catalogue → MongoDB documents (varying attributes per product).</p>`},{q:'Compare the four core NoSQL data models (Key-Value, Document, Column-Family, and Graph) using the Splendid Stores e-commerce platform from Lecture 4. For each model, name a representative database engine, show a concrete query/command example, explain which Splendid Stores workload it fits best, and discuss why Document stores scale out horizontally more easily than Graph databases.',model:`<p><b>Background:</b> Modern e-commerce platforms handle diverse workloads — from sub-millisecond shopping cart updates and polymorphic product catalogs to high-volume order history logs and multi-hop social recommendations. Using a single database engine for all four workloads forces painful performance or schema compromises, motivating <b>polyglot persistence</b> across the four core NoSQL data models.</p><p><b>Answer and solution:</b></p><ol><li><b>Key-Value Store (e.g., Redis):</b><ul><li><b>Data model:</b> Maps a unique key to an opaque value or hash structure with $O(1)$ lookup latency.</li><li><b>Splendid Stores workload &amp; query:</b> Ephemeral shopping carts keyed by user ID: <code>HSET cart:3 7:2 2</code>, <code>HINCRBY cart:3 7:2 1</code>, <code>HGETALL cart:3</code>, and <code>EXPIRE cart:3 86400</code>.</li><li><b>Limitation:</b> Cannot efficiently query across values (e.g., finding all carts containing a specific product).</li></ul></li><li><b>Document Database (e.g., MongoDB):</b><ul><li><b>Data model:</b> Self-contained JSON/BSON documents with nested objects and arrays, supporting secondary indexes on any internal field.</li><li><b>Splendid Stores workload &amp; query:</b> Product catalog with embedded variations: <code>db.products.find({"variations.price": {$lt: 3000}}, {name: 1, _id: 0})</code>.</li></ul></li><li><b>Column-Family / Wide-Column Store (e.g., Apache Cassandra):</b><ul><li><b>Data model:</b> Rows organized into partitions keyed by a <b>partition key</b> (which determines node placement) and sorted on disk by <b>clustering columns</b>.</li><li><b>Splendid Stores workload &amp; query:</b> Massive write-heavy order history queried by customer: <code>PRIMARY KEY ((user_id), order_id)</code> allowing single-partition reads for <code>SELECT * FROM orders_by_user WHERE user_id = 3;</code>.</li></ul></li><li><b>Graph Database (e.g., Neo4j using Cypher / ISO GQL):</b><ul><li><b>Data model:</b> Nodes and directed relationships stored with index-free adjacency for fast multi-hop traversals.</li><li><b>Splendid Stores workload &amp; query:</b> Collaborative filtering ("customers who bought what Adaeze bought also bought"):<pre>MATCH (a:User {name: "Adaeze"})-[:BOUGHT]-&gt;(:Product)&lt;-[:BOUGHT]-(:User)-[:BOUGHT]-&gt;(rec:Product)
WHERE NOT EXISTS { MATCH (a)-[:BOUGHT]-&gt;(rec) }
RETURN DISTINCT rec.name;</pre></li></ul></li></ol><p><b>Scenario:</b> Suppose Splendid Stores grows to 50 million users across 16 database nodes. When sharding the <b>MongoDB</b> product and order collections by <code>_id</code> or <code>user_id</code>, each document is a self-contained aggregate, so fetching a product or a user's order reads from a single shard. By contrast, if the <b>Neo4j</b> purchase graph is split across 16 servers, a 3-hop recommendation traversal from Adaeze to shared products to other users must repeatedly cross network boundaries between machines.</p><p><b>How they combine:</b> In a polyglot architecture, PostgreSQL acts as the ACID system of record for financial checkout and stock deduction, while events stream out to Redis (active session carts), MongoDB (catalog search), Cassandra (append-only order timeline), and Neo4j (recommendation engine).</p><p><b>Conclusion:</b> Document and Column-Family stores scale out cleanly because their data is partitioned around self-contained aggregates or partition keys, whereas Graph databases excel at deep relationship traversals on tightly coupled nodes where horizontal sharding introduces network-hop bottlenecks.</p>`,rubric:['Identifies all four models with a representative engine (Redis, MongoDB, Cassandra, Neo4j)','Provides concrete Splendid Stores queries/commands for each (HSET/HGETALL/EXPIRE, db.products.find, PRIMARY KEY ((user_id), order_id), and Cypher MATCH recommendation pattern)','Explains why documents shard cleanly as independent aggregates whereas sharding graphs cuts edges and causes cross-server network hops during traversals'],pts:['Identifies all four models with a representative engine (Redis, MongoDB, Cassandra, Neo4j)','Provides concrete Splendid Stores queries/commands for each (HSET/HGETALL/EXPIRE, db.products.find, PRIMARY KEY ((user_id), order_id), and Cypher MATCH recommendation pattern)','Explains why documents shard cleanly as independent aggregates whereas sharding graphs cuts edges and causes cross-server network hops during traversals']}]},
{id:'t7',title:'MongoDB basics',why:'CRUD and query operators in the Mongo shell.',
cards:[
{h:'CRUD',b:`<pre>db.students.insertOne({name:"Ada", level:400, courses:["CSC420"]})
db.students.find({level: {$gte: 300}}, {name:1, _id:0})
db.students.updateOne({name:"Ada"}, {$set:{level:500}})
db.students.updateOne({name:"Ada"}, {$push:{courses:"SEN408"}})
db.students.deleteOne({name:"Ada"})
db.students.aggregate([{$group:{_id:"$level", n:{$sum:1}}}])</pre><p>Database → collection → document (BSON); every document has <code>_id</code>. Operators: $eq, $gt, $gte, $lt, $in, $and, $or.</p>`},
{h:'Relational to MongoDB Mapping, BSON & Core Limits',b:`<table><tr><th>Relational (PostgreSQL)</th><th>MongoDB (Document Model)</th></tr><tr><td>Database</td><td>Database</td></tr><tr><td>Table</td><td><b>Collection</b> (created lazily on first insert; no <code>CREATE TABLE</code> required)</td></tr><tr><td>Row / Tuple</td><td><b>BSON Document</b> (Binary JSON)</td></tr><tr><td>Column</td><td><b>Field</b> (key-value pair; documents in one collection can have different fields)</td></tr><tr><td>Primary Key</td><td><b><code>_id</code></b> (mandatory, unique per collection, immutable; auto-generated as a 12-byte <code>ObjectId</code> if omitted)</td></tr><tr><td>Foreign Key Constraint</td><td><b>Not enforced by MongoDB</b> (deleting a parent document leaves orphan references unless application code cleans them up)</td></tr></table><ul><li><b>Why BSON instead of plain JSON?</b> Fast binary length-prefixed scanning plus extra native types: <code>ObjectId</code>, <code>Date</code> (<code>ISODate</code>), 32/64-bit ints, <code>Decimal128</code>, and binary data.</li><li><b>Hard Limits &amp; Defaults:</b> Max single document size = <b>16 MiB</b>; <code>mongod</code> default port = <b>27017</b> (<code>mongodb://localhost:27017</code>); interactive shell = <b><code>mongosh</code></b>; GUI = <b>MongoDB Compass</b> (includes an embedded <code>_MONGOSH</code> tab); managed cloud = <b>MongoDB Atlas</b>.</li></ul>`},
{h:'Reshaping Splendid Stores (7 Tables to 4 Collections) & Embed vs Reference',b:`<p>In Lecture 5, the 7 relational tables of <code>splendid_stores</code> collapse into <b>4 MongoDB collections</b>:</p><ol><li><b><code>manufacturers</code> (13 docs):</b> Keeps explicit integer <code>_id</code> (1..13), <code>name</code>, <code>country</code>.</li><li><b><code>products</code> (15 docs):</b> Absorbs <code>variations</code> as an <b>embedded array of subdocuments</b> and keeps <code>tags</code> as an array, while storing <code>manufacturer_id</code> as a <b>reference</b>.</li><li><b><code>users</code> (10 docs):</b> Absorbs <code>addresses</code> as an <b>embedded subdocument</b> <code>address: {street, city, state}</code>.</li><li><b><code>orders</code> (14 docs):</b> Uses order string as <code>_id</code> (e.g., <code>"20260801-3"</code>), references <code>user_id</code>, and embeds <code>items: [...]</code>.</li></ol><table><tr><th>Embed Subdocuments When...</th><th>Reference by <code>_id</code> When...</th></tr><tr><td>1-to-1 or 1-to-few data owned by the parent (e.g., user <code>address</code>, product <code>variations</code>)</td><td>Shared lookup data updated in one place (e.g., <code>manufacturers</code> — embedding would require updating 100s of products when a manufacturer changes country)</td></tr><tr><td>Always read/updated together (single-document writes in MongoDB are <b>atomic</b>)</td><td>Unbounded growth (e.g., a user's lifetime <code>orders</code> could exceed the <b>16 MiB</b> document size limit)</td></tr></table>`},
{h:'Shell Basics, Projections & Dot Notation vs Exact Subdocument Match',b:`<p><b>Core <code>mongosh</code> commands:</b> <code>show dbs</code>, <code>use splendid_stores</code>, <code>show collections</code>, <code>db.products.countDocuments()</code>, <code>db.products.findOne()</code>.</p><ul><li><b><code>db.coll.find(filter, projection)</code>:</b> <code>1</code> includes a field, <code>0</code> excludes it. You cannot mix inclusion and exclusion in one projection — <b>except for <code>_id: 0</code></b>, because <code>_id</code> is always returned by default unless explicitly suppressed.</li><li><b>Dot Notation Trap (requires quotes!):</b><ul><li><code>db.users.find({ "address.city": "Lagos" })</code> matches every user whose embedded <code>address</code> has <code>city == "Lagos"</code> regardless of other address fields.</li><li><code>db.users.find({ address: { city: "Lagos" } })</code> returns <b>0 documents</b> because without dot notation MongoDB checks for an <b>exact whole-subdocument match</b> (a document whose <code>address</code> has <i>only</i> the <code>city</code> field and no <code>street</code> or <code>state</code>)!</li></ul></li></ul>`},
{h:'CRUD & Update Operators: $set, $unset, $inc & upsert',b:`<ul><li><b>Insert:</b> <code>insertOne({...})</code> and <code>insertMany([{...}, {...}])</code>.</li><li><b>Update (requires an atomic operator!):</b><ul><li><code>$set</code>: sets or creates fields: <code>db.users.updateOne({_id: 10}, {$set: {phone: "080..."}})</code></li><li><code>$unset</code>: removes a field completely (value passed does not matter, usually <code>""</code> or <code>1</code>): <code>db.customers.updateOne({CustomerID: 10}, {$unset: {Email: ""}})</code> (Exam Q4c-ii).</li><li><code>$inc</code>: increments/decrements a number (if the field does not exist yet, <code>$inc</code> <b>creates it</b> starting from 0 + delta): <code>db.users.updateOne({_id: 3}, {$inc: {loyalty_points: 50}})</code></li></ul></li><li><b><code>updateOne</code> vs <code>updateMany</code> / <code>deleteOne</code> vs <code>deleteMany</code>:</b> <code>*One</code> modifies only the first matching document; <code>*Many</code> modifies all matching documents.</li><li><b>Upsert (<code>{ upsert: true }</code>):</b> Passed as the 3rd argument to <code>updateOne</code>/<code>updateMany</code>. If a document matches the filter, it updates it (<code>matchedCount: 1, modifiedCount: 1</code>); if zero documents match, it combines the filter + update fields and inserts a new document (<code>matchedCount: 0, upsertedCount: 1, upsertedId: ...</code>).</li></ul>`},
{h:'Query Conditionals, distinct() Two-Step Join & The $not vs $lte Trap',b:`<ul><li><b>Comparison operators:</b> <code>$gt</code>, <code>$gte</code>, <code>$lt</code>, <code>$lte</code>, <code>$ne</code>, <code>$in: [...]</code>, <code>$nin: [...]</code>, and date comparisons with <code>ISODate("2026-08-01")</code>.</li><li><b>Logical OR:</b> <code>db.products.find({ $or: [{ manufacturer_id: 1 }, { tags: "dairy" }] })</code></li><li><b>Two-Step Referenced Query with <code>distinct()</code> (Lecture 6 Slide 17):</b> Why can't you pass <code>const some_users = db.users.find({"address.state": "Lagos"})</code> into <code>db.orders.find({user_id: {$in: some_users._id}})</code>? Because <code>find()</code> returns a <b>Cursor object</b> (not an array of IDs), so <code>some_users._id</code> is missing! Instead, use <b><code>distinct()</code></b>, which returns a plain JavaScript array of values:<pre>const lagos_ids = db.users.distinct("_id", { "address.state": "Lagos" });
// lagos_ids is [ 1, 7, 10 ]
db.orders.find({ user_id: { $in: lagos_ids } });</pre></li></ul><div class="trap"><b>The <code>$not</code> vs <code>$lte</code> Trap (Lecture 6 Slide 19):</b> Suppose only 1 out of 10 users has a <code>loyalty_points</code> field (value <code>150</code>), and the other 9 users do not have the field at all.<br>• <code>db.users.countDocuments({ loyalty_points: { $lte: 100 } })</code> returns <b><code>0</code></b> (only compares existing numeric fields).<br>• <code>db.users.countDocuments({ loyalty_points: { $not: { $gt: 100 } } })</code> returns <b><code>9</code></b> because <b><code>$not</code> also matches documents where the field does not exist!</b></div>`},
{h:'Array Queries: $all, $size & The $elemMatch Mixed-Elements Trap',b:`<ul><li><b><code>$all</code>:</b> Matches arrays containing all specified elements regardless of order: <code>{ tags: { $all: ["beverage", "breakfast"] } }</code>.</li><li><b><code>$size</code>:</b> Matches arrays with an exact element count: <code>{ variations: { $size: 3 } }</code>. (<b>Note:</b> <code>$size</code> does <b>not</b> accept range operators like <code>$gt</code>; to query array length by range, store a separate counter field or use aggregation).</li></ul><div class="trap"><b>Array of Subdocuments Trap — Dot Notation vs <code>$elemMatch</code> (Lecture 6 Slides 25–26):</b><br>• <code>db.products.find({ "variations.price": { $lt: 5000 }, "variations.qty_on_hand": { $lt: 100 } })</code> checks whether <i>any</i> variation in the array has <code>price &lt; 5000</code> AND <i>any</i> (possibly different!) variation has <code>qty_on_hand &lt; 100</code>. It falsely matches <b>Nestle Milo</b> where 400g tin has <code>price: 3800, qty: 120</code> and 800g refill has <code>price: 6800, qty: 75</code>!<br>• To require a <b>single element</b> of the array to satisfy both conditions simultaneously, use <b><code>$elemMatch</code></b>:<pre>db.products.find({
  variations: { $elemMatch: { price: { $lt: 5000 }, qty_on_hand: { $lt: 100 } } }
})</pre></div>`},
{h:'Array Projections ($slice, $) & Array Update Operators ($push, $addToSet, $pull)',b:`<ul><li><b>Array Projections:</b><ul><li><code>{ variations: { $slice: 1 } }</code> returns only the <b>first</b> variation in the array; <code>{ $slice: -1 }</code> returns only the <b>last</b> variation.</li><li><code>db.products.find({ "variations.price": { $gt: 10000 } }, { name: 1, "variations.$": 1 })</code> uses the <b>positional projection operator <code>"variations.$"</code></b> to return only the <b>first matching element</b> from the array.</li></ul></li><li><b>Array Update Operators:</b><ul><li><b><code>$push</code>:</b> Appends an element to the end of an array (even if it is a duplicate): <code>{$push: {tags: "promo"}}</code>.</li><li><b><code>$addToSet</code>:</b> Appends an element to an array <b>only if it is not already present</b> (prevents duplicates!): <code>{$addToSet: {tags: "beverage"}}</code>.</li><li><b><code>$pull</code>:</b> Removes all array elements matching a value or condition: <code>{$pull: {tags: "promo"}}</code> or <code>{$pull: {variations: {variation_id: 4}}}</code>.</li><li><b>Positional update <code>$</code>:</b> Updates the first array element matched by the query filter: <code>db.products.updateOne({_id: 1, "variations.variation_id": 2}, {$set: {"variations.$.price": 7000}})</code>.</li></ul></li></ul>`}],
recall:[['MongoDB hierarchy.','Database → collection → document.'],['Which operator adds an element to an array field?','$push.'],['Which operator updates specific fields?','$set.'],['What is the maximum size of a single BSON document in MongoDB, and what default TCP port does mongod listen on?','16 MiB (16 megabytes) is the maximum BSON document size, and 27017 is the default TCP port for mongod.'],['Why does db.users.find({ address: { city: "Lagos" } }) return 0 documents when users have address: { street: "...", city: "Lagos", state: "Lagos" }, and how do you fix it?','Without dot notation, { address: { city: "Lagos" } } checks for an exact whole-subdocument match where address has ONLY the city field. You fix it by quoting the dot-notation path: db.users.find({ "address.city": "Lagos" }).'],['In Lecture 6, why does db.users.countDocuments({ loyalty_points: { $not: { $gt: 100 } } }) return 9 while { loyalty_points: { $lte: 100 } } returns 0?','Because $not matches both documents where loyalty_points <= 100 AND documents where the loyalty_points field does not exist at all (9 users lack the field), whereas $lte only matches documents where the field exists and is <= 100.'],['Why should you use db.users.distinct("_id", { "address.state": "Lagos" }) instead of db.users.find(...) when building a two-step $in filter for db.orders.find()?','db.users.find() returns a Cursor object rather than an array of IDs. db.users.distinct("_id", filter) returns a plain JavaScript array of matching IDs (e.g., [1, 7, 10]) that can be passed directly into { user_id: { $in: lagos_ids } }.'],['Explain the difference between querying an array of subdocuments with dot notation vs $elemMatch.','Dot notation ({"variations.price": {$lt: 5000}, "variations.qty_on_hand": {$lt: 100}}) matches a document if ANY element satisfies the first condition and ANY (possibly different) element satisfies the second. $elemMatch ({variations: {$elemMatch: {price: {$lt: 5000}, qty_on_hand: {$lt: 100}}}}) requires a SINGLE array element to satisfy all conditions simultaneously.'],['What is the difference between $push and $addToSet in MongoDB array updates?','$push always appends the item to the array even if it already exists, whereas $addToSet only appends the item if it is not already present in the array.'],['In Exam Q4(d)(iv), what problem arises when importing a JSON file of Meals or Orders into MongoDB if none of the JSON objects contain an _id field?','MongoDB will auto-generate a random 12-byte ObjectId as _id for every document instead of using MealID or OrderID as the primary key. As a result, MealID/OrderID uniqueness is not enforced (re-importing creates duplicate meals/orders) and lookups by _id will not match MealID/OrderID.']],
quiz:[{k:'mcq',q:'Find students with level in [300, 400]:',o:['{level:{$in:[300,400]}}','{level:[300,400]}','{$in:level}','{level:{$all:300}}'],a:0},{k:'gap',q:'Every MongoDB document has a unique ___ field.',a:'_id',alt:['id']},{q:'Which MongoDB query correctly finds products that have at least ONE variation whose `price` is less than `5000` AND whose own `qty_on_hand` is less than `100`?',o:['db.products.find({ "variations.price": { $lt: 5000 }, "variations.qty_on_hand": { $lt: 100 } })','db.products.find({ variations: { $elemMatch: { price: { $lt: 5000 }, qty_on_hand: { $lt: 100 } } } })','db.products.find({ variations: { $all: [{ price: 5000 }, { qty_on_hand: 100 }] } })','db.products.find({ "variations.$": { price: { $lt: 5000 }, qty_on_hand: { $lt: 100 } } })'],a:1,e:'`$elemMatch` ensures that the same single subdocument inside `variations` satisfies both `price < 5000` and `qty_on_hand < 100`.',k:'mcq',w:'`$elemMatch` ensures that the same single subdocument inside `variations` satisfies both `price < 5000` and `qty_on_hand < 100`.'},{q:'In MongoDB, what does `db.customers.updateOne({ CustomerID: 10 }, { $unset: { Email: "" } })` do (Exam Q4c-ii)?',o:['It sets the `Email` field of customer 10 to an empty string `""`.','It completely removes (drops) the `Email` field from the first customer document where `CustomerID` is `10`.','It deletes the entire customer 10 document from the `customers` collection.','It drops the `Email` column from every document in the `customers` collection.'],a:1,e:'The `$unset` operator deletes the specified field (`Email`) from the matched document; with `updateOne`, only the customer with `CustomerID: 10` is modified.',k:'mcq',w:'The `$unset` operator deletes the specified field (`Email`) from the matched document; with `updateOne`, only the customer with `CustomerID: 10` is modified.'},{q:'Which projection returns ONLY the `name` field and suppresses the `_id` field in `db.products.find({}, ...)`?',o:['{ name: 1 }','{ name: 1, _id: 0 }','{ name: true, _id: null }','{ $project: { name: 1 } }'],a:1,e:'In `find(filter, projection)`, `_id` is always included by default unless explicitly excluded with `_id: 0`.',k:'mcq',w:'In `find(filter, projection)`, `_id` is always included by default unless explicitly excluded with `_id: 0`.'},{q:'What happens when you execute `db.users.updateOne({ _id: 5 }, { $inc: { loyalty_points: 100 } })` on a user document that does NOT currently have a `loyalty_points` field?',o:['MongoDB throws a missing-field error.','MongoDB ignores the update and sets `modifiedCount: 0`.','MongoDB creates the `loyalty_points` field on user 5 and sets its value to `100`.','MongoDB inserts a brand-new user document with `_id: 100`.'],a:2,e:'When `$inc` targets a non-existent field on a matched document, MongoDB creates the field and initializes it to the increment amount (`0 + 100 = 100`).',k:'mcq',w:'When `$inc` targets a non-existent field on a matched document, MongoDB creates the field and initializes it to the increment amount (`0 + 100 = 100`).'},{q:'Why did the Lecture 5 schema design for `splendid_stores` keep `manufacturers` as a separate referenced collection instead of embedding manufacturer details inside every product document?',o:['Because MongoDB forbids embedding objects inside documents.','Because multiple products share the same manufacturer; referencing avoids duplicating manufacturer data across products and prevents update anomalies if a manufacturer\'s details change.','Because a single manufacturer object exceeds the 16 MiB BSON limit.','Because MongoDB enforces foreign key constraints on `manufacturer_id`.'],a:1,e:'Shared master entities (1-to-many where many products share one manufacturer) are referenced by `_id` to avoid duplication and multi-document update anomalies.',k:'mcq',w:'Shared master entities (1-to-many where many products share one manufacturer) are referenced by `_id` to avoid duplication and multi-document update anomalies.'}],
theory:[{q:'Write MongoDB commands to insert a product, find products under ₦5,000 sorted by price, raise one product\'s price, and count products per category.',pts:['insertOne','find with $lt + sort','updateOne $set/$inc','aggregate $group $sum'],model:`<pre>db.products.insertOne({name:"Pen", price:300, category:"Stationery"})
db.products.find({price:{$lt:5000}}).sort({price:1})
db.products.updateOne({name:"Pen"}, {$inc:{price:50}})
db.products.aggregate([{$group:{_id:"$category", count:{$sum:1}}}])</pre>`},{q:'Exam Q4 (PAUFs Meal Delivery Service in MongoDB): Given three collections Customers(CustomerID, Name, Email, Location), Orders(OrderID, CustomerID, OrderDate, TotalAmount), and Meals(MealID, Name, Category, NutritionalInfo): (a) Explain what makes the document model semi-structured with examples (3m). (b) State what db.meals.find() does and write its PostgreSQL equivalent (2.5m). (c) Write MQL commands to: (i) insert a customer without an email address (2m); (ii) drop the Email field of the customer with CustomerID 10 (2.5m). (d) Write MQL commands to: (i) insert two new meals in the "Salad" category matching Figure 2 (3m); (ii) delete all meals in the "Salad" category (1.5m); (iii) update OrderID 1003 to set TotalAmount to 750 (2.5m); and (iv) explain the potential problem with importing Figure 2\'s JSON documents when none of them specify an _id field (3m).',model:`<p><b>Background:</b> PAUFs uses MongoDB to store customer profiles, meal catalogs with nested nutritional metrics, and customer orders. Unlike PostgreSQL's schema-on-write tables, MongoDB stores BSON documents in schema-flexible collections manipulated through the MongoDB Query Language (MQL).</p><p><b>Answer and solution:</b></p><p><b>(a) Why the Document Model is Semi-Structured (3 marks):</b></p><ul><li>Documents are <b>self-describing</b> (every field stores both its key name and its BSON data type alongside the value) and <b>polymorphic</b> (documents inside the same collection do not need to share the exact same fields).</li><li><i>Example 1:</i> One document in <code>customers</code> can omit the <code>Email</code> field while another includes <code>Email</code> and an extra <code>Phone</code> array.</li><li><i>Example 2:</i> In <code>meals</code>, <code>NutritionalInfo</code> is a nested subdocument <code>{Calories: 450, Protein: "35g", Carbs: "20g", Fat: "25g"}</code> whose internal keys could vary across meal types (e.g., adding <code>Fiber</code> or <code>Allergens</code> to specific meals without running <code>ALTER TABLE</code>).</li></ul><p><b>(b) Meaning of <code>db.meals.find()</code> and PostgreSQL Equivalent (2.5 marks):</b></p><ul><li><code>db.meals.find()</code> (with an empty filter) retrieves a cursor over <b>all documents and all fields</b> in the <code>meals</code> collection.</li><li><b>PostgreSQL equivalent:</b> <code>SELECT * FROM meals;</code></li></ul><p><b>(c) Customer Operations (4.5 marks):</b></p><pre>// (i) Insert a customer without an Email field (2 marks)
db.customers.insertOne({
  CustomerID: 11,
  Name: "Adaeze Okafor",
  Location: "Lekki, Lagos"
});

// (ii) Drop (remove) the Email field of the customer with CustomerID 10 (2.5 marks)
db.customers.updateOne(
  { CustomerID: 10 },
  { $unset: { Email: "" } }
);</pre><p><b>(d) Meal and Order Operations &amp; Import Analysis (10 marks):</b></p><pre>// (i) Insert two new meals for the "Salad" category matching Figure 2 (3 marks)
db.meals.insertMany([
  {
    MealID: 103,
    Name: "Greek Chicken Salad",
    Category: "Salad",
    NutritionalInfo: { Calories: 320, Protein: "28g", Carbs: "12g", Fat: "18g" }
  },
  {
    MealID: 104,
    Name: "Avocado Caesar Salad",
    Category: "Salad",
    NutritionalInfo: { Calories: 380, Protein: "15g", Carbs: "18g", Fat: "26g" }
  }
]);

// (ii) Delete all meals of the "Salad" category (1.5 marks)
db.meals.deleteMany({ Category: "Salad" });

// (iii) Update OrderID 1003 to have TotalAmount of 750 (2.5 marks)
db.orders.updateOne(
  { OrderID: 1003 },
  { $set: { TotalAmount: 750 } }
);</pre><p><b>(d)(iv) Potential Problem Importing Figure 2 Without an <code>_id</code> Field (3 marks):</b></p><ul><li>In Figure 2, documents define business keys like <code>"MealID": 101</code> and <code>"OrderID": 1001</code>, but omit MongoDB's primary key <code>"_id"</code>.</li><li>When imported via <code>mongoimport</code> or <code>insertMany</code>, MongoDB automatically generates a new random 12-byte <code>ObjectId</code> for <code>_id</code> on every document. Consequently, <b>MongoDB will not enforce uniqueness on <code>MealID</code>, <code>OrderID</code>, or <code>CustomerID</code></b> — re-running the import or inserting another meal with <code>MealID: 101</code> succeeds and creates duplicate records, and primary-key index lookups on <code>_id</code> cannot be used to join or look up by <code>MealID</code>/<code>CustomerID</code> unless unique indexes are manually created or those IDs are mapped to <code>_id</code> (as done in Lecture 5's <code>splendid_stores</code>).</li></ul><p><b>Scenario:</b> If a staff member imports <code>meals.json</code> twice during setup, the collection ends up with two separate documents both having <code>MealID: 101</code> ("Grilled Chicken Salad") under different auto-generated <code>_id</code> values, causing <code>updateOne({MealID: 101}, ...)</code> to update only one copy while leaving the duplicate stale.</p><p><b>How they combine:</b> Parts (a)–(c) show how MongoDB handles schema evolution dynamically via optional fields on <code>insertOne</code> and field removal via <code>$unset</code>. Part (d) combines batch writes (<code>insertMany</code>, <code>deleteMany</code>) and targeted updates (<code>$set</code>) with physical primary-key design (<code>_id</code> vs domain IDs).</p><p><b>Conclusion:</b> While MongoDB's semi-structured document model makes adding or dropping fields effortless, production collections should still assign domain identifiers to <code>_id</code> (or create explicit unique indexes) to prevent duplicate imports.</p>`,rubric:['Part (a): Explains self-describing BSON fields, optional/missing fields across documents, and nested subdocuments like NutritionalInfo','Part (b): States db.meals.find() returns all documents in meals and equates it to SELECT * FROM meals;','Part (c): Uses insertOne without Email for (c-i) and updateOne({CustomerID: 10}, {$unset: {Email: ""}}) for (c-ii)','Part (d): Uses insertMany with two Salad documents containing nested NutritionalInfo, deleteMany({Category: "Salad"}), updateOne({OrderID: 1003}, {$set: {TotalAmount: 750}}), and explains that omitting _id causes auto-generated ObjectIds and allows duplicate MealID/OrderID records'],pts:['Part (a): Explains self-describing BSON fields, optional/missing fields across documents, and nested subdocuments like NutritionalInfo','Part (b): States db.meals.find() returns all documents in meals and equates it to SELECT * FROM meals;','Part (c): Uses insertOne without Email for (c-i) and updateOne({CustomerID: 10}, {$unset: {Email: ""}}) for (c-ii)','Part (d): Uses insertMany with two Salad documents containing nested NutritionalInfo, deleteMany({Category: "Salad"}), updateOne({OrderID: 1003}, {$set: {TotalAmount: 750}}), and explains that omitting _id causes auto-generated ObjectIds and allows duplicate MealID/OrderID records']}]}
],
past:{title:'CSC420 Feb 2026 Examination & CA1 Practical Test',meta:'2025/2026 Semester 1 • Final Exam (2 Hours: Section A [25m] + Section B any 2 of 3 [40m]) & CA1 Practical Test (30m)',qs:[{id:'exam26-q1',num:'Exam Q1 (25m)',topic:'t1',marks:25,q:'<p><b>Section A — Question 1 [25 Marks]:</b></p><p><b>(a) [10 marks]</b> Consider the <code>university_db</code> database with tables <code>instructor(ID, name, dept_name, salary)</code> and <code>department(dept_name, building, budget)</code>. Write a SQL transaction that onboards a new instructor <b>Stephen Adubi</b> (ID <code>\'90001\'</code>) into the <code>\'Comp. Sci.\'</code> department with a salary of <code>120000.00</code>, and immediately increases the <code>\'Comp. Sci.\'</code> department\'s budget by that same salary amount. Then explain which <b>ACID</b> properties are demonstrated if someone attempts to insert a negative salary that violates a <code>CHECK (salary &gt; 0)</code> constraint during a transaction.</p><p><b>(b) [6 marks]</b> Write the SQL command to create an index on <code>instructor(name)</code>. Explain how this B+-tree index improves lookup performance compared to a sequential scan, and describe a concrete query scenario where PostgreSQL would choose a <b>Bitmap Index Scan</b> instead of a regular Index Scan.</p><p><b>(c) [9 marks]</b> Construct a <b>B+-tree of degree (order) $n = 3$</b> (maximum 2 keys and 3 pointers per node) by inserting the following 8 instructor names in exact order: <code>\'Wu\', \'Einstein\', \'Katz\', \'Singh\', \'Alimi\', \'Crick\', \'Brandt\', \'Gold\'</code>. Show the tree after each split and trace how the range query <code>WHERE name BETWEEN \'Alimi\' AND \'Gold\'</code> is executed.</p>',model:`<p><b>(a) Onboarding Transaction &amp; ACID Analysis (10 marks):</b></p><pre>BEGIN;

INSERT INTO instructor (ID, name, dept_name, salary)
VALUES ('90001', 'Stephen Adubi', 'Comp. Sci.', 120000.00);

UPDATE department
SET budget = budget + 120000.00
WHERE dept_name = 'Comp. Sci.';

COMMIT;</pre><ul><li><b>Consistency:</b> The <code>CHECK (salary &gt; 0)</code> constraint enforces a domain integrity rule. Rejecting a negative salary prevents the database from transitioning into an illegal state.</li><li><b>Atomicity:</b> Because both the <code>INSERT</code> and <code>UPDATE</code> are wrapped inside one transaction block (<code>BEGIN ... COMMIT</code>), when the negative salary triggers a constraint error, PostgreSQL transitions the transaction to the <b>Failed</b> and then <b>Aborted</b> state, rolling back all changes so neither the invalid instructor nor any budget change persists ("all-or-nothing").</li></ul><p><b>(b) Index Creation, B+-Tree Performance &amp; Bitmap Scan Scenario (6 marks):</b></p><pre>CREATE INDEX idx_instructor_name ON instructor (name);</pre><ul><li><b>How B+-tree improves performance:</b> Instead of a <b>Sequential Scan</b> reading every heap page in $O(N)$ time, the balanced B+-tree navigates from the root to the target leaf in $O(\\log_b N)$ page reads and follows the leaf's TID (Tuple ID) directly to the heap page.</li><li><b>Bitmap Index Scan Scenario:</b> When a query matches <b>too many rows for random single-row I/O to be efficient, but too few rows for a full Sequential Scan</b> — or when <b>combining two different indexes</b> with <code>AND</code>/<code>OR</code> (e.g., <code>WHERE dept_name = 'Comp. Sci.' OR salary &gt; 100000</code>). PostgreSQL runs a <b>Bitmap Index Scan</b> to set bits in an in-memory bitmap of TIDs ( combining multiple bitmaps with bitwise <code>BitmapAnd</code>/<code>BitmapOr</code>), sorts the matched page numbers, and performs a <b>Bitmap Heap Scan</b> reading each disk page sequentially once.</li></ul><p><b>(c) Degree-3 B+-Tree Construction &amp; Range Query Trace (9 marks):</b></p><ol><li><b>Insert <code>Wu</code>, <code>Einstein</code>:</b> Single leaf <code>[Einstein | Wu]</code>.</li><li><b>Insert <code>Katz</code> (overflow &rarr; split 1):</b> <code>[Einstein, Katz, Wu]</code> splits into left leaf <code>[Einstein]</code> and right leaf <code>[Katz | Wu]</code>; copy-up middle key <code>Katz</code> to new root <code>[Katz]</code>.</li><li><b>Insert <code>Singh</code>:</b> Goes right of <code>Katz</code> into <code>[Katz | Wu]</code> (3rd key overflow) &rarr; splits right leaf into <code>[Katz]</code> and <code>[Singh | Wu]</code>; push/copy up <code>Singh</code> into root <code>[Katz | Singh]</code>. Leaves: <code>[Einstein] &rarr; [Katz] &rarr; [Singh | Wu]</code>.</li><li><b>Insert <code>Alimi</code>:</b> Goes left of <code>Katz</code> into <code>[Einstein]</code> &rarr; leaf becomes <code>[Alimi | Einstein]</code>.</li><li><b>Insert <code>Crick</code> (overflow &rarr; leaf split + root split):</b> Goes into <code>[Alimi | Einstein]</code> &rarr; sorted <code>[Alimi, Crick, Einstein]</code> splits into <code>[Alimi]</code> and <code>[Crick | Einstein]</code>, promoting <code>Crick</code> to root. Root temporarily has 3 keys <code>[Crick, Katz, Singh]</code> &rarr; internal root splits! Middle key <b><code>Katz</code></b> moves up to a new Level-2 root <code>[Katz]</code> with left internal child <code>[Crick]</code> and right internal child <code>[Singh]</code>.</li><li><b>Insert <code>Brandt</code>:</b> Navigates <code>&lt; Katz</code>, <code>&lt; Crick</code> into leaf <code>[Alimi]</code> &rarr; becomes <code>[Alimi | Brandt]</code>.</li><li><b>Insert <code>Gold</code>:</b> Navigates <code>&lt; Katz</code>, <code>&gt;= Crick</code> into leaf <code>[Crick | Einstein]</code> &rarr; sorted <code>[Crick, Einstein, Gold]</code> splits into <code>[Crick]</code> and <code>[Einstein | Gold]</code>; promotes <code>Einstein</code> into left internal node <code>[Crick | Einstein]</code>.</li></ol><p><b>Final 3-Level B+-Tree ($n=3$):</b></p><ul><li><b>Root:</b> <code>[Katz]</code></li><li><b>Internal Level:</b> Left <code>[Crick | Einstein]</code> &nbsp;|&nbsp; Right <code>[Singh]</code></li><li><b>Linked Leaves:</b> <code>[Alimi | Brandt] &rarr; [Crick] &rarr; [Einstein | Gold] &rarr; [Katz] &rarr; [Singh | Wu]</code></li><li><b>Range Query <code>BETWEEN 'Alimi' AND 'Gold'</code>:</b> Traverse root <code>[Katz]</code> (go left) &rarr; internal <code>[Crick | Einstein]</code> (go left) &rarr; land on leaf <code>[Alimi | Brandt]</code>. Read <code>Alimi, Brandt</code>, follow sibling pointer <code>&rarr; [Crick]</code>, follow sibling pointer <code>&rarr; [Einstein | Gold]</code>, and stop before <code>Katz</code>.</li></ul>`,h:'Exam Q1 (25m)',pts:['Complete model solution verified against marking scheme']},{id:'exam26-q2',num:'Exam Q2 (25m)',topic:'t3',marks:25,q:'<p><b>Section A — Question 2 [25 Marks] (Alternative to Q1):</b></p><p>You are designing a PostgreSQL database for an <b>Online Marketplace</b>:</p><p><b>(a) [10 marks]</b> Create a composite type <code>contact_info</code> with fields <code>(name TEXT, email TEXT, phone TEXT, address TEXT)</code>. Create a table <code>users(user_id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY, profile contact_info, preferences TEXT[])</code>. Write SQL statements to: (i) insert two users with composite <code>profile</code> values and <code>preferences</code> arrays; (ii) update a user\'s phone number inside <code>profile</code>; and (iii) delete any user whose <code>profile.address</code> is <code>\'Test Location\'</code>.</p><p><b>(b) [7 marks]</b> Create a <code>products</code> table with a <code>tags TEXT[]</code> column. Write SQL statements to insert a product tagged with <code>ARRAY[\'smartphone\', \'android\', \'wireless\']</code>, retrieve all products that contain <code>\'wireless\'</code> in their tags, and append a new tag <code>\'5G\'</code> to an existing product.</p><p><b>(c) [8 marks]</b> Add a <code>specs JSONB</code> column to <code>products</code>. Insert a product with JSONB specifications including <code>{"brand": "Samsung", "ram": "8GB", "battery": "5000mAh"}</code>, and write a query using the JSONB containment operator to find all products whose <code>specs</code> contain a <code>"5000mAh"</code> battery.</p>',model:`<p><b>(a) Composite Type <code>contact_info</code> and CRUD Operations (10 marks):</b></p><pre>-- 1. Create composite type and users table
CREATE TYPE contact_info AS (
  name    TEXT,
  email   TEXT,
  phone   TEXT,
  address TEXT
);

CREATE TABLE users (
  user_id     SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
  profile     contact_info,
  preferences TEXT[]
);

-- (i) Insert two users using ROW()
INSERT INTO users (profile, preferences) VALUES
  (ROW('Adaeze Okafor', 'adaeze@pau.edu.ng', '08031112222', 'Lekki, Lagos'),
   ARRAY['electronics', 'books']),
  (ROW('Chidi Nwosu', 'chidi@test.com', '08093334444', 'Test Location'),
   ARRAY['fashion', 'gaming']);

-- (ii) Update phone field inside composite column (NO parentheses on target column!)
UPDATE users
SET profile.phone = '08039998888'
WHERE user_id = 1;

-- (iii) Delete user where profile.address is 'Test Location' (Parentheses REQUIRED in WHERE!)
DELETE FROM users
WHERE (profile).address = 'Test Location';</pre><p><b>(b) Array Column <code>tags TEXT[]</code> Operations (7 marks):</b></p><pre>CREATE TABLE products (
  product_id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
  name       TEXT NOT NULL,
  price      NUMERIC(10, 2) NOT NULL,
  tags       TEXT[]
);

-- Insert product with tags array
INSERT INTO products (name, price, tags)
VALUES ('Galaxy A55', 450000.00, ARRAY['smartphone', 'android', 'wireless']);

-- Query products containing 'wireless' (using ANY or @>)
SELECT * FROM products
WHERE 'wireless' = ANY(tags);
-- Equivalent: WHERE tags @> ARRAY['wireless'];

-- Append '5G' to tags
UPDATE products
SET tags = array_append(tags, '5G')
WHERE product_id = 1;</pre><p><b>(c) JSONB Column <code>specs</code> and Containment Query (8 marks):</b></p><pre>ALTER TABLE products ADD COLUMN specs JSONB;

INSERT INTO products (name, price, tags, specs)
VALUES (
  'Galaxy M54',
  520000.00,
  ARRAY['smartphone', 'android', 'wireless'],
  '{"brand": "Samsung", "ram": "8GB", "battery": "5000mAh"}'::jsonb
);

-- Find products with a 5000mAh battery using JSONB containment @>
SELECT product_id, name, price, specs
FROM products
WHERE specs @&gt; '{"battery": "5000mAh"}'::jsonb;
-- Alternative text extraction: WHERE specs-&gt;&gt;'battery' = '5000mAh';</pre>`,h:'Exam Q2 (25m)',pts:['Complete model solution verified against marking scheme']},{id:'exam26-q3',num:'Exam Q3 (20m)',topic:'t4',marks:20,q:'<p><b>Section B — Question 3 [20 Marks]:</b></p><p><b>(a) [5 marks]</b> State the differences between a SQL function and a SQL stored procedure.</p><p><b>(b) [9 marks]</b> Using the <code>student(id, name, dept_name, dob)</code> table: (i) write a PL/pgSQL function <code>check_voting_eligibility(p_student_id VARCHAR)</code> that returns <code>\'Eligible\'</code> if the student is at least 18 years old and <code>\'Not Eligible\'</code> otherwise [6 marks]; (ii) write a SQL function that returns the <code>name</code>, <code>dob</code>, <code>age</code>, and voting eligibility of all students in the <code>\'Physics\'</code> department by invoking your function from b(i) [3 marks].</p><p><b>(c) [6 marks]</b> Study the table-returning function <code>instructor_of(dname text) RETURNS TABLE(id varchar, name varchar, dept_name varchar, salary numeric)</code> in Figure 1, which returns all instructors in department <code>dname</code>. Given the table <code>mentoring(studentid, instructorid)</code>: (i) write a SQL query to fetch the <b>IDs of the mentees of the lowest earner in each department</b> [4 marks]; and (ii) state the tweak needed if the <b>names</b> of the mentees are also required [2 marks].</p>',model:`<p><b>(a) Function vs Stored Procedure (5 marks):</b></p><ol><li><b>Invocation:</b> Functions are called inside SQL query expressions (<code>SELECT fn(x)</code>), whereas procedures are invoked with the standalone <code>CALL proc(x);</code> command.</li><li><b>Return Value:</b> Functions must declare a <code>RETURNS</code> clause and return a scalar or table result; procedures do not return a value (except optionally via <code>INOUT</code> parameters).</li><li><b>Transaction Control:</b> Stored procedures can execute <code>COMMIT</code> and <code>ROLLBACK</code> inside their body; functions execute inside the caller's transaction and cannot commit or roll back.</li><li><b>Role:</b> Functions compute values or table views; procedures execute multi-statement transactional workflows.</li></ol><p><b>(b)(i) PL/pgSQL Function <code>check_voting_eligibility</code> (6 marks):</b></p><pre>CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION check_voting_eligibility(p_student_id VARCHAR)
RETURNS TEXT AS $$
DECLARE
  v_age INT;
BEGIN
  SELECT EXTRACT(YEAR FROM AGE(CURRENT_DATE, dob))::INT
  INTO v_age
  FROM student
  WHERE id = p_student_id;

  IF NOT FOUND THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'Student % not found', p_student_id;
  END IF;

  IF v_age &gt;= 18 THEN
    RETURN 'Eligible';
  ELSE
    RETURN 'Not Eligible';
  END IF;
END;
$$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;</pre><p><b>(b)(ii) SQL Function for Physics Department Students (3 marks):</b></p><pre>CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION physics_students_eligibility()
RETURNS TABLE(name VARCHAR, dob DATE, age INT, eligibility TEXT) AS $$
  SELECT
    s.name,
    s.dob,
    EXTRACT(YEAR FROM AGE(CURRENT_DATE, s.dob))::INT AS age,
    check_voting_eligibility(s.id) AS eligibility
  FROM student s
  WHERE s.dept_name = 'Physics';
$$ LANGUAGE sql;</pre><p><b>(c)(i) Query for Mentee IDs of the Lowest Earner in Each Department (4 marks):</b></p><pre>SELECT
  i.dept_name,
  i.id AS instructor_id,
  m.studentid AS mentee_id
FROM instructor i
JOIN mentoring m ON m.instructorid = i.id
WHERE i.salary = (
  SELECT MIN(sub.salary)
  FROM instructor_of(i.dept_name) AS sub
);</pre><p><b>(c)(ii) Tweak to Also Fetch Mentee Names (2 marks):</b></p><p>Join the <code>student</code> table on the mentee's student ID (<code>JOIN student s ON s.id = m.studentid</code>) and add <code>s.name AS mentee_name</code> to the <code>SELECT</code> clause.</p>`,h:'Exam Q3 (20m)',pts:['Complete model solution verified against marking scheme']},{id:'exam26-q4',num:'Exam Q4 (20m)',topic:'t7',marks:20,q:'<p><b>Section B — Question 4 [20 Marks] (MongoDB — PAUFs Meal Delivery):</b></p><p>PAUFs uses three MongoDB collections: <code>customers(CustomerID, Name, Email, Location)</code>, <code>orders(OrderID, CustomerID, OrderDate, TotalAmount)</code>, and <code>meals(MealID, Name, Category, NutritionalInfo)</code>.</p><p><b>(a) [3 marks]</b> Explain what makes the document model semi-structured, giving examples from PAUFs.</p><p><b>(b) [2.5 marks]</b> State what <code>db.meals.find()</code> does and write its equivalent command in PostgreSQL.</p><p><b>(c) [4.5 marks]</b> Write MongoDB commands to: (i) insert a customer without an email address [2m]; (ii) drop the <code>Email</code> field of the customer with ID 10 [2.5m].</p><p><b>(d) [10 marks]</b> Write MongoDB commands to: (i) insert two new meals for the <code>"Salad"</code> category matching Figure 2\'s structure [3m]; (ii) delete all meals of the <code>"Salad"</code> category [1.5m]; (iii) update the order with <code>OrderID: 1003</code> to have a <code>TotalAmount</code> of <code>750</code> [2.5m]; and (iv) state a potential problem with importing Figure 2\'s JSON data where no document specifies an <code>_id</code> field [3m].</p>',model:`<p><b>(a) Semi-Structured Document Model (3 marks):</b></p><p>In a document database, each BSON document is self-describing (storing field names and types alongside values) and collections do not enforce a fixed schema-on-write. Different documents in <code>customers</code> can omit optional fields like <code>Email</code> or add extra fields, and documents in <code>meals</code> can embed nested subdocuments like <code>NutritionalInfo: {Calories: 450, Protein: "35g", Carbs: "20g", Fat: "25g"}</code> with varying attributes per meal category.</p><p><b>(b) <code>db.meals.find()</code> &amp; PostgreSQL Equivalent (2.5 marks):</b></p><p><code>db.meals.find()</code> returns a cursor containing all documents and all fields in the <code>meals</code> collection. Its PostgreSQL equivalent is <code>SELECT * FROM meals;</code>.</p><p><b>(c) Customer Insert &amp; Field Drop (4.5 marks):</b></p><pre>// (i) Insert customer without Email (2m)
db.customers.insertOne({
  CustomerID: 11,
  Name: "Tunde Bakare",
  Location: "Ajah, Lagos"
});

// (ii) Drop Email field of customer with ID 10 (2.5m)
db.customers.updateOne(
  { CustomerID: 10 },
  { $unset: { Email: "" } }
);</pre><p><b>(d) Meals &amp; Orders CRUD + Missing <code>_id</code> Analysis (10 marks):</b></p><pre>// (i) Insert two new Salad meals (3m)
db.meals.insertMany([
  {
    MealID: 103,
    Name: "Mediterranean Salad",
    Category: "Salad",
    NutritionalInfo: { Calories: 290, Protein: "12g", Carbs: "15g", Fat: "20g" }
  },
  {
    MealID: 104,
    Name: "Tuna Garden Salad",
    Category: "Salad",
    NutritionalInfo: { Calories: 360, Protein: "30g", Carbs: "10g", Fat: "21g" }
  }
]);

// (ii) Delete all Salad meals (1.5m)
db.meals.deleteMany({ Category: "Salad" });

// (iii) Update OrderID 1003 TotalAmount to 750 (2.5m)
db.orders.updateOne(
  { OrderID: 1003 },
  { $set: { TotalAmount: 750 } }
);</pre><p><b>(iv) Problem Importing Figure 2 Without <code>_id</code> (3m):</b> Because none of the JSON documents specify <code>_id</code>, MongoDB auto-generates a random <code>ObjectId</code> for <code>_id</code> on every imported document instead of using <code>MealID</code>, <code>OrderID</code>, or <code>CustomerID</code> as the primary key. Consequently, uniqueness is not enforced on <code>MealID</code>/<code>OrderID</code> (re-importing duplicates every meal and order), and primary-key index lookups on <code>_id</code> cannot be used for <code>MealID</code>/<code>CustomerID</code> references.</p>`,h:'Exam Q4 (20m)',pts:['Complete model solution verified against marking scheme']},{id:'exam26-q5',num:'Exam Q5 (20m)',topic:'t1',marks:20,q:'<p><b>Section B — Question 5 [20 Marks]:</b></p><p><b>(a) [7.5 marks]</b> Textually and pictorially describe the <b>five possible states</b> of a database transaction.</p><p><b>(b) [12.5 marks]</b> Given the E-Commerce schema in Figure 3 with <code>products(product_id, product_info, price, manufacturer_id)</code> and <code>orders(orderid, customer_id, orderdetails jsonb, totalamount, orderdate)</code>, and the call in Figure 4:</p><pre>CALL make_orders(\n  3,\n  \'[{"product_id": 7, "quantity": 1}, {"product_id": 9, "quantity": 4}, {"product_id": 11, "quantity": 2}]\'::jsonb\n);</pre><p>Write: (i) a helper function <code>compute_total(p_items JSONB)</code> that looks up each product\'s price from <code>products</code> and calculates the total order amount [7.5 marks]; and (ii) the stored procedure <code>make_orders(p_user_id INT, p_items JSONB)</code> that constructs <code>orderid</code> by concatenating the current timestamp with <code>p_user_id</code>, calls your helper function for <code>totalamount</code>, and inserts the row into <code>orders</code> [5 marks].</p>',model:`<p><b>(a) Five States of a Database Transaction (7.5 marks):</b></p><pre>                    +-------------------+
                    |Partially Committed| -----&gt; [ Committed ]
                    +-------------------+
                       ^             |
  (final statement)    |             | (commit/disk failure)
                       |             v
[ Active ] ------------+------&gt; [ Failed ] -----&gt; [ Aborted ]
           (error / constraint / abort)         (rollback done)</pre><ol><li><b>Active:</b> Initial state while the transaction's read and write statements are executing.</li><li><b>Partially Committed:</b> Reached right after the final statement finishes executing in memory, before all log records are flushed to disk.</li><li><b>Failed:</b> Entered from <i>Active</i> or <i>Partially Committed</i> when a constraint violation, runtime error, explicit <code>ROLLBACK</code>, or hardware/power failure prevents normal completion.</li><li><b>Aborted:</b> Entered after the recovery manager undoes (rolls back) all changes made by the failed transaction, restoring the database to its prior consistent state.</li><li><b>Committed:</b> Entered from <i>Partially Committed</i> once the commit record and WAL entries are durably flushed to non-volatile storage.</li></ol><p><b>(b)(i) Helper Function <code>compute_total(p_items JSONB)</code> (7.5 marks):</b></p><pre>CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION compute_total(p_items JSONB)
RETURNS NUMERIC(12, 2) AS $$
DECLARE
  v_total NUMERIC(12, 2);
BEGIN
  SELECT COALESCE(
    SUM(p.price * (elem-&gt;&gt;'quantity')::INT),
    0
  )
  INTO v_total
  FROM jsonb_array_elements(p_items) AS elem
  JOIN products p
    ON p.product_id = (elem-&gt;&gt;'product_id')::INT;

  RETURN v_total;
END;
$$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;</pre><p><b>(b)(ii) Stored Procedure <code>make_orders(p_user_id INT, p_items JSONB)</code> (5 marks):</b></p><pre>CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE make_orders(p_user_id INT, p_items JSONB)
LANGUAGE plpgsql AS $$
DECLARE
  v_orderid TEXT;
  v_total   NUMERIC(12, 2);
BEGIN
  v_orderid := TO_CHAR(NOW(), 'YYYYMMDDHH24MISS') || '-' || p_user_id::TEXT;
  v_total   := compute_total(p_items);

  INSERT INTO orders (orderid, customer_id, orderdetails, totalamount, orderdate)
  VALUES (v_orderid, p_user_id, p_items, v_total, NOW());
END;
$$;</pre>`,h:'Exam Q5 (20m)',pts:['Complete model solution verified against marking scheme']},{id:'ca1-q1-q7',num:'CA1 Practical (30m)',topic:'t5',marks:30,q:'<p><b>CSC420 CA1 Practical Test (SEN) — <code>business_db</code> [30 Marks]:</b></p><p>Tables: <code>customers(customer_id, customer_name, segment, city, state_code, acct_balance, interests TEXT[])</code>, <code>products(product_id, product_name, category, unit_price)</code>, <code>orders(order_id, customer_id, product_id, order_date, status, quantity_ordered, order_total, deliveries INT[])</code>.</p><ul><li><b>Q1 [2m]:</b> List <code>customer_name, city, state_code, acct_balance</code> for <code>\'Corporate\'</code> customers in <code>\'KAN\'</code>, ordered by <code>acct_balance</code> descending.</li><li><b>Q2 [3m]:</b> For <code>\'Completed\'</code> orders, show each product <code>category</code>, total quantity ordered, and total revenue, ordered by total revenue descending.</li><li><b>Q3 [4m]:</b> List <code>customer_id, customer_name, segment, state_code</code> of customers who have never placed an order, ordered by <code>customer_id</code>.</li><li><b>Q4 [5m]:</b> Using a CTE, list products (<code>product_id, product_name, category, product_revenue, category_avg_revenue</code>) whose total completed-order revenue exceeds the average product revenue of their category.</li><li><b>Q5 [4m]:</b> Create a B+-tree index to optimize queries filtering by <code>status = \'Completed\'</code> and a date range on <code>order_date</code>.</li><li><b>Q6 [6m]:</b> (1) For every customer interested in <code>\'Groceries\'</code>, remove <code>\'Groceries\'</code> and add <code>\'Laptops\'</code> and <code>\'Phones &amp; Tablets\'</code>. (2) Find the top 5 interests among <code>\'Wholesale\'</code> customers.</li><li><b>Q7 [6m]:</b> Produce a single-row 2025 summary (<code>total_orders, total_quantity_ordered, total_quantity_delivered, incomplete_orders</code>) for <code>\'Electronics\'</code> products ordered by customers in <code>\'LAG\'</code>.</li></ul>',model:`<pre>-- Q1 [2 marks]
SELECT customer_name, city, state_code, acct_balance
FROM customers
WHERE state_code = 'KAN' AND segment = 'Corporate'
ORDER BY acct_balance DESC;

-- Q2 [3 marks]
SELECT p.category,
       SUM(o.quantity_ordered) AS total_quantity,
       SUM(o.order_total)      AS total_revenue
FROM orders o
JOIN products p USING (product_id)
WHERE o.status = 'Completed'
GROUP BY p.category
ORDER BY total_revenue DESC;

-- Q3 [4 marks]
SELECT c.customer_id, c.customer_name, c.segment, c.state_code
FROM customers c
LEFT JOIN orders o USING (customer_id)
WHERE o.order_id IS NULL
ORDER BY c.customer_id;

-- Q4 [5 marks]
WITH product_rev AS (
  SELECT p.product_id,
         p.product_name,
         p.category,
         COALESCE(SUM(o.order_total), 0) AS product_revenue
  FROM products p
  LEFT JOIN orders o
    ON o.product_id = p.product_id AND o.status = 'Completed'
  GROUP BY p.product_id, p.product_name, p.category
),
cat_stats AS (
  SELECT *,
         AVG(product_revenue) OVER (PARTITION BY category) AS category_avg_revenue
  FROM product_rev
)
SELECT product_id, product_name, category,
       ROUND(product_revenue, 2)      AS product_revenue,
       ROUND(category_avg_revenue, 2) AS category_avg_revenue
FROM cat_stats
WHERE product_revenue &gt; category_avg_revenue
ORDER BY category, product_revenue DESC;

-- Q5 [4 marks]
CREATE INDEX idx_orders_status_date ON orders (status, order_date);

-- Q6-1 [3 marks]
UPDATE customers
SET interests = array_cat(
  array_remove(interests, 'Groceries'),
  ARRAY['Laptops', 'Phones &amp; Tablets']
)
WHERE 'Groceries' = ANY(interests);

-- Q6-2 [3 marks]
SELECT interest, COUNT(*) AS customer_count
FROM customers,
LATERAL unnest(interests) AS interest
WHERE segment = 'Wholesale'
GROUP BY interest
ORDER BY customer_count DESC, interest ASC
LIMIT 5;

-- Q7 [6 marks]
SELECT
  COUNT(*) AS total_orders,
  COALESCE(SUM(o.quantity_ordered), 0) AS total_quantity_ordered,
  COALESCE(SUM((
    SELECT COALESCE(SUM(x), 0) FROM unnest(o.deliveries) AS x
  )), 0) AS total_quantity_delivered,
  COUNT(*) FILTER (
    WHERE COALESCE((
      SELECT SUM(x) FROM unnest(o.deliveries) AS x
    ), 0) &lt; o.quantity_ordered
  ) AS incomplete_orders
FROM orders o
JOIN customers c USING (customer_id)
JOIN products p USING (product_id)
WHERE p.category = 'Electronics'
  AND c.state_code = 'LAG'
  AND o.order_date &gt;= DATE '2025-01-01'
  AND o.order_date &lt;  DATE '2026-01-01';</pre>`,h:'CA1 Practical (30m)',pts:['Complete model solution verified against marking scheme']}]}
};
